WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:12.160
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 5 of CME 295.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.280
So today is an exciting
day because we'll

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.400
be talking about LLM
systems and, in particular,

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.640
how we can train and serve
large language models at scale.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.080
But before we do that,
I want to first motivate

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.280
why we would want to do that.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.800
So today is lecture
5, which means we

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.260
had four lectures before that.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.400
And during those
four lectures, we

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.800
had a buildup where we saw the
foundations of LLMs and the fact

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.400
that they were based
on the transformer.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.760
And we saw that
LLMs in particular,

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.200
they frame these problems
as text-to-text problems.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.920
And in particular,
what they do is

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.150
they take the decoder
part of transformers,

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.270
and they scale that up.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:07.390
And in particular, they scale
that in terms of model size.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.430
So LLMs typically have a size
on the order of billions,

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.430
if not trillions.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.990
So it is huge.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.550
And then the second
aspect, among others,

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.070
is they're trained
on a lot of data.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.790
So we had seen that,
for instance, there

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.670
is this very expensive stage
called pre-training, where

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.830
these models are trained
on trillions of tokens

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.430
or even tens of
trillions of tokens.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:41.610
So all of that means that we
need to have the hardware.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.310
We need to have
infrastructure that

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:51.110
allows us to serve these models
but also train these models.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.270
And the key question
is, well, how can

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.930
we make this work in practice?

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.550
So all of that was
theoretical, but how

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.590
can we actually make that work?

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:06.590
So as you all know, if
we look at under the hood

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:12.630
of the decoder, we actually have
a relatively simple architecture

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.150
that is composed
of two main layers.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.910
So the first one is the
masked multi-head attention,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.230
and the second one is the
feedforward neural network.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.430
But if you think about
it, these two layers,

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:34.710
they're actually just a bunch
of matrix multiplications.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.610
And so I know we're not
even two minutes in,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.550
but I have a question for you.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.150
What is the piece
of hardware that

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:47.390
is excellent at matrix
multiplications?

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.790
GPUs, yes, exactly.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:53.390
So the key of this
lecture is to see

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:57.500
how we can leverage these
GPUs in a smart way.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.820
And by the way, I'm
not sure if you all

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.740
know why GPUs are very
relevant for this kind of task.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:10.060
So GPUs, they have a
lot of cores that allow

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.940
you to parallelize operations.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.860
And so if you think about
matrix multiplications,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:21.780
let's assume you
have some matrix A,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.860
let's say with some
number of rows,

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:28.900
and some matrix B with
some number of columns.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.780
If you were to compute the
result of this operation,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:39.820
each element can be computed
in a parallel fashion.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:45.580
So for instance, this element is
just this dot product of this.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:52.020
This one is this dot
product of this, and so on.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.850
So everything can be
nicely parallelized

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:59.010
and GPUs actually have a
lot of cores that allow

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.130
you to do that in parallel.

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:06.210
They actually have specialized
hardware to do that very well.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.930
So with that being
said, I want us

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:14.810
to talk about what
features these GPUs have.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.810
But in order to do that, we
need to have some metrics.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.290
So if you remember
in lecture 2, we

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.190
are talking about how
we could scale LLMs.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:28.090
And we had mentioned two
metrics that people use.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:32.690
So we had said we have the
number of floating point

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.430
operations, which is
a measure of computes,

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:38.810
and in particular,
compute budget.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.410
And this one is
typically spelled

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:44.530
as flops with a
little S. And we had

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.010
said there is
another flops which

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.290
people can confuse very easily.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:53.330
There is actually a
measure of compute speed.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:57.170
So flops either with
a big S or with a /s.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:01.770
So this one is floating
point operations per second.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.610
So that one is a measure of how
performant your hardware is.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:08.930
And I believe we
had not actually

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:10.970
used that second metric.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:12.930
But today, we'll be using it.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.610
So just as an FYI.

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:20.450
So if we were to take a
look at a very good GPU

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.730
out there, so the
H200 from NVIDIA,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:28.690
you would have a datasheet
that looks like this.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:32.290
You would have a bunch
of characteristics that

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:35.090
characterize the compute speed.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.830
And as you can see here, it's
measured as a function of flops.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:42.130
So teraflops in this case.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:46.670
Then you also have some
indication of how much memory

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.850
this device has.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.120
And in particular,
we will see that this

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.120
is something we can use to store
computations, either temporary

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.720
or ones we want to use
to update our model.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.680
And then we have
also some indication

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:10.360
of the speed of how we can
transfer these pieces of data.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:16.400
And here, we can see it's on the
order of terabytes per second.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.720
So the problem with this is--

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:27.000
I mentioned a few slides ago
that we want to serve and train

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:31.080
huge models on the order of
billions of parameters, hundreds

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.840
of billions of parameters.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.660
And as with all
pieces of hardware,

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:39.240
these numbers are quite limited.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.640
And in particular, the memory,
which is used for us to,

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:48.080
let's say, for instance, store
the parameters of our model,

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.730
it's only on the order
of hundreds of gigabytes.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:57.110
So in this case, 141
gigabyte, to be more precise.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:01.150
So if we were to do
some napkin math,

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:07.650
let's assume you have a model
of size 100 billion parameters.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:11.610
And if you have, let's say,
2 bytes per parameter--

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:13.870
and by the way, this is,
I think, a fair assumption

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:19.910
because let's suppose you store
your parameters in floating

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:24.350
point 16, which is a way to
store your parameter at a given

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:25.130
granularity.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.830
You actually are storing
this in 16 bits, which

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:32.030
is equal to 2 bytes.

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:34.270
So if you do that,
then that means

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:39.150
that just the fact of
storing your parameters

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:43.590
will cost you 200
gigabytes of memory, which

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:47.810
is greater than the memory
limit of the GPU that we saw,

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:51.790
which, by the way,
is a very good GPU.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:54.470
And on top of that--

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:56.970
so this is just to
have the parameters.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.750
But on top of that, for
training, what you need to do

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:04.190
is to actually train your
model, learn on its mistakes,

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:06.470
and update the weights.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:11.310
And if you want to do
that, well, you don't just

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.330
store your parameters.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.590
You also need to store, for
instance, the activations.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:17.410
You need to store,
your gradients.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.830
You need to store
the optimizer states.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:23.270
And all of that is
a lot of memory.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:28.830
So anyways, I hope I convinced
you that GPU memory is limited.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.070
And this is the
first limitation.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:35.669
And the second limitation
is that in order

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:40.210
to use these models, well,
it's going to be expensive.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.190
On the training
side, you'll have

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:44.790
a bunch of tokens you
want to train this on.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.660
And on the inference
side, well, maybe you

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:50.300
have a lot of requests
you want to serve.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:54.980
And this is the reason why,
throughout this lecture,

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:58.820
we'll try to answer two
key questions, which

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:03.020
are, how do we
make the model fit

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:05.500
within the piece of hardware?

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.080
And the second question
is, how do we make it fast?

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:13.660
Cool.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:17.700
And the way we're going
to approach this is we're

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.260
first going to see at the
GPU level, like within a GPU,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:27.020
if there are things we can do in
order to answer these questions.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.660
So we're first going to
start with a method that

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:34.340
is called flash
attention that aims

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:38.420
at improving how we
perform these operations

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:40.420
on a single GPU.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:44.100
And we're going to see that
this method is an exact method.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.890
We're not making
any approximations.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:48.650
We're just performing
the computations

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:53.890
in a way that is making
things more efficient.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:58.370
So I mentioned right
before that LLMs

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.710
are composed of decoders
from the transformers.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:05.570
And I mentioned that there
are two layers in the decoder.

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:07.530
One is the masked
self-attention,

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:10.210
and the other one is the
feedforward neural network.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:16.050
Well, if you take a look at the
masked self-attention layer,

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.850
what you see is
that you actually

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:25.890
need to compute and potentially
store a huge matrix.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:30.150
So that layer is where you
perform your self-attention.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:33.790
So each token attends
to everyone else

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:37.890
or rather, in the case of the
decoder, to itself and the ones

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:39.970
before it.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:45.370
And here, you need to have
Q times K transpose, which

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:49.770
is the dot product of all your
queries with all the other keys.

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:55.850
And n here is the
sequence length.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:57.650
So it can be big.

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.610
It can be tens of thousands.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:04.610
So even for, let's say, 1,000,
1,000 times 1,000 is a million.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:09.130
So you'd have 1 million entries
that you need to populate

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:14.410
for just a sequence
length of 1,000 tokens.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:19.410
And this, well,
is very expensive

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:21.890
to store in particular.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:25.770
So let's see how we
can actually do this.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:30.490
So if we take a look
at a GPU, we actually

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:32.170
see that there are
a few components

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:34.170
that we're leveraging.

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:36.490
The first component
that I want to call out

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:44.280
is called the HBM, which stands
for high bandwidth memory,

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:49.200
and it is the kind of memory
where the memory size was

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.300
posted in a few slides before.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:53.840
So the memory that you
see here is actually

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.640
the memory from the HBM.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:01.600
So that one is a
kind of memory that

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.120
has enough space to have big
intermediary computations.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.640
And it is relatively fast.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:12.600
I mean, on the absolute
scale, it's fast.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.440
But on a relative scale
compared to the SRAM,

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.880
it is relatively slow.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.100
And what do I mean by slow?

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:25.880
I mean by if you want to
read or write data to it,

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:34.440
it has roughly one order of
magnitude less transfer speeds.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:39.870
So you can think of the HBM as
a piece that has roughly, let's

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:45.250
say, one or so terabyte per
second of transfer speed,

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:50.990
whereas the SRAM, which is
another piece of memory,

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:54.510
has a much higher
transfer speed.

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:56.950
So you can access it on
the order of, let's say,

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:00.390
tens of terabyte per second.

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:04.650
And that piece of
memory, it's faster,

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:07.070
but it's also much smaller,
which means that you cannot

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:13.270
store these big entities
in that kind of memory.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:19.030
So in the case of the
self-attention, what you naively

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:26.190
need to do is to store that
huge matrix in the HBM, which

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.430
is big but also slow.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.790
And the key question
that we want to answer

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:36.030
is, is there any
way for us to avoid

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:39.580
storing that big
matrix in a place that

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:43.720
is slow to read and write into?

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:44.220
Yeah?

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.180
So the question is,
when you say you

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.440
have one 40 gigabytes of
memory, is it the HBM or SRAM?

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.940
So SRAM actually
has much less memory

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:57.900
on the order of, I believe,
hundreds of megabytes.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:02.880
And the 141 gigabytes is
actually from the HBM.

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:07.460
So it's the big and slow memory.

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:11.420
So one thing that I want
to say is that in a GPU,

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.500
computations are
happening in what

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:23.860
we call the compute unit, which
is on chip, and HBM is off chip.

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:27.020
So just a little detail.

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:31.200
So what we want to do
is, as I mentioned,

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:37.940
to limit the number of
reads and writes operations

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:40.460
to that big and slow components.

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:43.740
So let's see how we can do that.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:49.620
So the self-attention
operation involves an operator,

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:53.100
which is a softmax
operator, which

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:57.380
is defined as, if you
consider a vector,

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:01.860
the softmax of that vector
at a particular component

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:06.580
is the exponential
of that component

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:10.020
over the sum of the
exponential of all components

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:13.100
in that vector.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:18.520
And the problem is that operator
requires, in a naive way,

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:24.900
for you to compute all these
components across your vector.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:29.820
So in other words, you cannot
obtain softmax of x at component

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:35.450
i without processing
the whole row.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:37.410
You cannot.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:41.010
And the problem
with that is, given

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:44.410
that you need to process the
whole row, well, in order

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:49.570
to compute the self-attention
operation in a naive way, what

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:53.050
you would need to
do is to actually

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:57.570
load the query and
the key matrices

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:02.970
and to perform the
whole matrix operations,

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:06.370
and then write that to the HBM.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:11.050
And then you need to
read that whole matrix

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:16.530
and then perform the softmax
computation on the whole matrix

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.350
and write that to the HBM again.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:25.730
And then loads that
resulting quantity

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:29.010
and then multiply
that by your value

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:32.170
in order to obtain your output.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:35.880
So in other words, let me
just recap what we just said.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:38.880
The limitation of the
softmax is that you

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:44.180
need to compute that
sum over the whole row.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:45.640
So that assumes
that you actually

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:47.200
process the whole
row, which is why

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.840
you need to do all these
steps and do all these

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:54.080
reads and writes from the HBM.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:56.520
So the key question
that we want to ask

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:03.280
is, is there a way for us to not
have to process the whole row?

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:05.040
Is there a way for
us to actually do

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.200
that in an iterative fashion?

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:12.960
So this is the key question
that the flash attention

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:16.560
method tries to answer.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:22.079
So we're going to do some--

00:17:22.079 --> 00:17:25.119
I wouldn't call it math but,
yeah, some little writing

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:26.220
on the blackboards.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:39.880
So let's assume you have
a row vector s and then

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:45.000
a column vector v. So why
are we in this situation?

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.200
Well, it is because
if you have your,

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:53.820
let's say, query and
then key transpose,

00:17:53.820 --> 00:18:00.920
so the softmax operation of
this with some, let's say,

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.680
scaling factor times v.

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:11.000
So if you take your
query, which has

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.900
row vectors and
your key transpose,

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:25.280
which has some column vectors,
in order to have the attention

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:31.230
weights of a given query
and all the other keys,

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:36.350
you will perform these dot
products between your query

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:39.947
and all the other keys.

00:18:39.947 --> 00:18:41.530
So you would have
something like this.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:48.870
So for the first query, you
would have this times this.

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.430
So let's assume it's s1.

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:59.510
And then this times
this, it is s2, up to sn.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:04.830
And what you want to
do is to find a way

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:14.670
to compute softmax of this
vector times some scaling

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:20.990
factor times the values.

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:26.230
And the values here,
you can think of it

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:32.320
as the matrix with
some row vectors.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:39.100
So what we want
to figure out is,

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:46.420
is there a way for us to compute
the softmax times this vector

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:50.860
without having to compute
everything within here first,

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:56.060
and then compute the
softmax of all this thing

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:59.380
and multiply that by the vector?

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:02.100
This is what we
want to figure out.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:07.000
And so this is why we have a
simple example on the slide,

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:10.740
which is the row vector s,
which you can think of it

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:16.260
as just your attention
scores before the softmax

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:19.860
that you want to figure
out if you can compute

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:23.580
the softmax for that, times v.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:27.660
So in mathematical
terms, we want

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:33.980
to know if we can compute
softmax of s times v

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:36.900
in a smart way.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:41.080
So softmax of s times
v, in other words,

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:48.780
it is the weighted
sum of the values

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:52.380
as a function of the
attention scores, which

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.760
are softmax as you see.

00:20:54.760 --> 00:21:01.180
So it's exponential of the si's
over the sum of the exponential

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:02.520
of all the sj's.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:09.280
So far, does it make sense
what we're trying to achieve?

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:09.780
Yeah?

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.660
OK, great.

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.900
So this is our objective.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:18.100
And so as I mentioned, the
problem with this quantity

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:21.740
is that every
attention score needs

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:27.610
to have the full row processed
because the denominator has

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:32.490
the sum of the exponential
of all the components.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:38.330
But if you think about it
and if you put the sum symbol

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:41.410
at the numerator,
it just turns out

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:45.450
that you actually
have two quantities

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:50.210
that you could compute
in an iterative fashion.

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:55.410
So you have the numerator, which
is the sum of the exponential

00:21:55.410 --> 00:22:00.490
of the si's times the vi's
over the sum of the exponential

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:01.470
of the sj's.

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:13.450
So maybe one idea could be
to have an iterative fashion

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:17.970
to compute the numerator
and the denominator

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:23.840
so that we don't need to compute
the matrix within the softmax

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:26.520
and then the softmax
within the matrix

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:28.000
and then do the multiplication.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.120
We can do all at once.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:32.100
You can fuse the operations.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.620
And so this is what we're
going to see right now.

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:41.000
So in other words,
we're just noticing

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.600
that if we want
to express a given

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:45.880
vector as a function of
all the other vectors

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:49.120
by doing our attention
computation, which

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:53.360
is you interact the query
with all the other keys

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:57.520
that you softmax and you
multiply with the values,

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:03.920
you realize that this
expression is equal to something

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:06.760
over something else
that can be computed

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:08.100
in an iterative fashion.

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:15.320
And here we denote the
numerator and the denominator

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:16.900
with some quantities.

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:23.720
So nt and dt, where t
is the index up to which

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:27.180
we're constructing these sums.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:27.680
Yeah?

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:33.180
So the question is, why
are you doing it this way?

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:35.460
You can scale your
sum iteratively.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:36.940
So I think you
have the intuition.

00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:39.600
This is actually what
we're going to do.

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:43.440
So hold your thought,
let's say, five minutes.

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:45.460
We're going to see exactly
how we can do that.

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:50.980
Am I scaring you?

00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:54.600
Does it make sense?

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:55.400
Yeah?

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:56.000
OK.

00:23:56.000 --> 00:24:00.040
I'm saying this because
I think this methods,

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:03.460
beyond the actual thing
that we want to do,

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:06.600
I think the key thing is to
understand why we're doing it.

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:09.080
And the why we're
doing it here is

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:15.400
because we don't want to compute
the whole sum of the components

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:16.420
for all indices.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:19.160
We want to be able
to actually process

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:21.750
that in an iterative
fashion by fusing

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:25.270
all the operations, which
is what we're seeing here.

00:24:25.270 --> 00:24:33.150
So let's take a very simple
example of a row vector

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:37.270
with three elements, s1,
s2, s3, which we're assuming

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:42.150
is just a dot product
of q and k1, k2, k3.

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:45.990
So it's your query
attending to three keys.

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:49.870
And you want to know
how you can compute

00:24:49.870 --> 00:24:56.670
the average or the
weighted sum of how

00:24:56.670 --> 00:24:59.350
your query is similar
to the other keys

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:00.850
times the respective values.

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:03.350
That's what you want to compute.

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:07.870
So as we mentioned, the
way we want to compute this

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:10.030
is in an iterative
fashion because we

00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:15.310
saw that that formula can
be computed iteratively.

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:20.140
So we're going to start
with the first element,

00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:23.780
and we're going to see
that n1, the numerator,

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:29.860
is just equal to
exponential of s1 times v1.

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:33.740
The denominator is equal
to exponential of s1.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:36.980
So far, so good?

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:39.280
Then we proceed to
the second element.

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:42.940
So we perform s2 and v2.

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:47.100
The numerator is
exponential of s2 times v2,

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:51.460
and then the denominator
is exponential of s2.

00:25:51.460 --> 00:25:53.700
So far, we're just adding.

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:57.220
The third one is
exponential of s3 times v3

00:25:57.220 --> 00:25:59.720
and then exponential of s3.

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:06.340
In other words, we have
a simple relationship

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:13.980
between the expression of the
numerator up to index t plus 1

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:17.980
as a function of the
numerator up to index t,

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:24.860
and the element s at t
plus 1, and v at t plus 1.

00:26:24.860 --> 00:26:27.420
Same for dt plus 1.

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:32.380
And as a result of that,
you're able to compute

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:37.300
this whole expression
by just dividing

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:46.060
the n3 and d3 that we obtained
in an iterative fashion.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:52.980
So just note that
what we did is we

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:59.060
did not have to sum the whole
row and then to have that sum

00:26:59.060 --> 00:27:01.760
and then divide
everything with that sum.

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:06.540
We were able to actually perform
that operation iteratively

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:10.040
and obtain the results
in an iterative fashion.

00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:12.920
So I just want to emphasize
on the iterative nature of it.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:21.370
So, so far, I think
it's pretty easy.

00:27:21.370 --> 00:27:24.250
But there is one thing
in practice that happens,

00:27:24.250 --> 00:27:28.290
which is that when we're
handling exponentials,

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:33.250
the exponential of something
can be in, in fact, very big.

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:38.970
And it can lead to numerical
instabilities, which

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:42.290
is the reason why
what we want to do

00:27:42.290 --> 00:27:47.250
is to control how
big of the numbers

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:51.730
we're using to actually
perform the summation.

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:54.810
And in order to
control that, I first

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:58.570
want to say that we
first need to see

00:27:58.570 --> 00:28:03.370
that if you multiply the
numerator and the denominator

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:07.130
by the same quantity
by some constant, well,

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:10.450
that expression is exactly
equal to the numerator

00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:12.290
over the denominator.

00:28:12.290 --> 00:28:17.200
It's just a simple observation.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:22.120
So if you agree with my
statement, what that means is we

00:28:22.120 --> 00:28:27.120
just need to be smart in
choosing the constants by which

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:30.440
we're multiplying the
numerator and the denominator

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:37.560
to avoid us having to compute
big quantities that can lead

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:41.600
to numerical instabilities.

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:44.920
So everything that we
said so far is correct.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:48.280
Now, the thing that
we want to tweak

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:51.160
is making sure that
what we compute

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:53.895
is not numerically unstable.

00:28:53.895 --> 00:28:55.520
So in other words,
we want to make sure

00:28:55.520 --> 00:28:58.800
that these quantities
are not too big.

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:01.760
So how are we going to do that?

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:09.080
Well, one way is to multiply the
numerator and the denominator

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:15.360
with a constant that will
allow these quantities to not

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:16.440
be too big.

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:20.680
And that constant
could be exponential

00:29:20.680 --> 00:29:25.240
of minus the maximum
size that you

00:29:25.240 --> 00:29:32.280
have encountered up to the
element that you're processing.

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:34.980
So let's look at this
on a simple example.

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:37.160
So back to our example
with three elements.

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:38.640
Yeah?

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:41.360
So the question
is, wouldn't that

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:43.600
lead to another
instability, which

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:45.540
is that c could be equal to 0?

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:49.960
So you will see that actually
we're not computing C directly.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:54.400
We're going to use a property
of the exponential, which

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:57.040
is that if you
multiply an exponential

00:29:57.040 --> 00:30:00.820
by another exponential,
their exponent, they can add.

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:02.960
So you don't need to
actually compute that first.

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:05.040
You can compute what
is in the exponent

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:06.900
and then exponentiate that.

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:11.930
Any other questions here?

00:30:15.950 --> 00:30:17.090
OK, great.

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:19.013
But I think that's
a great point.

00:30:19.013 --> 00:30:20.930
And we're going to see
exactly how we do that.

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:22.950
So we said, what
we want to do is

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:29.110
to compute the ratio without
having to compute something that

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:29.810
is very big.

00:30:29.810 --> 00:30:32.750
So here, what we're
doing is we're

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:36.630
keeping track of the
biggest element we

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:39.710
have encountered so far.

00:30:39.710 --> 00:30:44.510
And we're storing that
in a variable called m,

00:30:44.510 --> 00:30:47.230
as in maximum.

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:52.110
So when we process the first
element the maximum element we

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:57.510
have encountered so far is s1.

00:30:57.510 --> 00:31:00.110
So here, if we go
about computing

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:04.030
the numerator and
the denominator,

00:31:04.030 --> 00:31:09.620
what we said is we want to make
sure that we're multiplying this

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:13.020
by this constant, which
is equal to exponential

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:19.220
of minus the maximum component
we have encountered so far.

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:25.740
And so here, it's nothing else,
that exponential of minus m1.

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:28.940
So if you do that, and
to answer your point,

00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:32.100
the nice thing with
exponentials is

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:34.460
if you multiply the
quantity in the numerator

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:36.940
and the denominator,
which are times

00:31:36.940 --> 00:31:40.140
exponentials with
an exponential,

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:44.020
then what that means is the
exponential becomes exponential

00:31:44.020 --> 00:31:46.900
of s1 minus m1.

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:48.780
And so here in the
order of things,

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:53.560
you would first compute s1 minus
m1 and then exponentiate that.

00:31:57.500 --> 00:32:01.540
So first iteration,
does that make sense?

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:03.620
Yeah?

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:04.160
OK.

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:06.420
So now, let's go to the
second iteration, which

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:09.860
is a little bit more tricky.

00:32:09.860 --> 00:32:14.140
Second iteration, we compute
the maximum of the elements

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:15.900
we have seen so far.

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:22.420
And so here we perform
max of s1 and s2,

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:25.020
so whichever is the biggest one.

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:27.420
And what we do is
actually something

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:30.460
a little bit more involved.

00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:36.500
So here, we are
multiplying that constant

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:38.340
on the numerator
and the denominator

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:40.440
of the element
that we just added,

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:44.220
which is the one
on the far right.

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:47.580
But then, and I think it
was back to your intuition,

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:53.220
we are scaling
everything we have summed

00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:59.220
so far with the new constant.

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:02.740
So actually, I think it
sounds more complicated

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:04.970
than what it actually is.

00:33:04.970 --> 00:33:07.870
So I'm just going to
tell you what that means.

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:13.310
So here, let's assume--

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:29.530
so the question is, what
happens if m2 is very big

00:33:29.530 --> 00:33:32.930
and m1 minus m2
becomes very small?

00:33:32.930 --> 00:33:36.110
So exponential of m1 minus
m2 becomes very small.

00:33:36.110 --> 00:33:37.577
And this becomes 0.

00:33:37.577 --> 00:33:38.910
Do you think that's the problem?

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:45.990
So the second term
would not be 0.

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:48.170
But the point that
I wanted to get at

00:33:48.170 --> 00:33:54.930
is if exponential of m1
minus m2 is very small,

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:58.610
what that means is
the second term was

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:01.090
meant to be very big anyways.

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:03.680
So if that one is
very small, then that

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:06.100
is just doing the thing
that it's supposed to do.

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:08.280
But I think maybe
your question was,

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:11.600
wouldn't we run into
numerical instabilities?

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:15.116
And in that case, well, if your
thing is very small and you

00:34:15.116 --> 00:34:16.699
cannot represent
something very small,

00:34:16.699 --> 00:34:18.980
it's not the end of the world.

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:21.560
It's just like approximately 0.

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:25.000
The thing that is very bad is if
you have something very big that

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:27.960
you cannot represent, because
that thing that is very big is

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:30.480
actually contributing
to your sum.

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:31.540
So that's not good.

00:34:31.540 --> 00:34:34.400
But if something is
very, very small,

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:37.760
if you cannot represent
it, you just set it to 0,

00:34:37.760 --> 00:34:40.560
and problem solved.

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:42.360
So here, what I
wanted to tell you

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:47.139
was why do we need to
rescale that component?

00:34:47.139 --> 00:34:52.760
Why do we need to rescale
the sum that we have computed

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:58.360
so far on top of adding
the constant to the element

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:01.160
that we just added?

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:04.990
So I wanted to tell you that
this is probably not something

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:09.170
that is extremely complicated.

00:35:09.170 --> 00:35:16.310
So let's assume you have your
numerator and your denominator.

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:23.590
And let's assume you had some
constants that you were using.

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:28.030
And then let's assume I
am multiplying that by,

00:35:28.030 --> 00:35:30.270
let's say, numerator so far.

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:31.270
OK, let me see.

00:35:31.270 --> 00:35:40.010
Maybe let's say
n1 and n2 and d1.

00:35:40.010 --> 00:35:42.970
I just want to make sure my
notations are not confusing.

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:45.550
So let's assume that you
were computing your numerator

00:35:45.550 --> 00:35:48.590
and denominator
iteratively, let's suppose,

00:35:48.590 --> 00:35:50.690
for the first iteration.

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:53.830
And then you had one constant
that you were applying there,

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:55.790
let's say C1.

00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:57.430
And now let's assume
that you're adding

00:35:57.430 --> 00:36:03.310
a new elements on the
numerator and the denominator.

00:36:03.310 --> 00:36:04.670
Let's assume you're adding--

00:36:09.310 --> 00:36:14.430
OK, not great, n2 and d2
lowercase, because these are not

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:15.830
sums.

00:36:15.830 --> 00:36:22.270
So here, if you adjust
these quantities with c2,

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:25.910
the problem is if
you do the ratio,

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:27.410
you're going to
have a problem here.

00:36:27.410 --> 00:36:29.870
It's not the same constant.

00:36:29.870 --> 00:36:36.950
So here, what you want to do
is to adjust the constants

00:36:36.950 --> 00:36:44.950
in a way that makes c2 be the
constant for the whole sum.

00:36:44.950 --> 00:36:46.710
So here, what are
we going to do?

00:36:46.710 --> 00:36:48.630
You're going to simply--

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:55.890
so for this example,
do over c1 times c2.

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:02.860
Over c1-- sorry.

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:03.980
c1.

00:37:03.980 --> 00:37:12.100
So you compute this portion, and
you multiply it by c2 over c1.

00:37:12.100 --> 00:37:14.660
So the constant that you're
using here over the constant

00:37:14.660 --> 00:37:17.980
that you were using, which
is exactly the element

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:21.060
in green on the slide.

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:26.780
So exponential of m1 minus
m2 is your second constant

00:37:26.780 --> 00:37:28.800
over your first constant.

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:33.060
Because here, we
were saying that c1

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:36.780
was equal to
exponential of minus m1,

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:41.560
and c2 is equal to
exponential of minus m2.

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:46.500
And we said it is
now c2 over c1,

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:56.180
which is equal to exponential
of m1 minus m2, which

00:37:56.180 --> 00:37:59.010
is what is on the slides.

00:37:59.010 --> 00:38:01.690
Does that make sense?

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:06.730
So I think this is
probably the hardest part.

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:09.390
I'm just reiterating here
that we have two elements.

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:11.150
The first one is the
new scaling factor.

00:38:11.150 --> 00:38:14.130
And the second one, which is the
rescaling factor of everything

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:16.730
we have computed so far.

00:38:16.730 --> 00:38:22.890
And we obtain these
identities for us

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:26.330
to iterate on the numerator
and the denominator.

00:38:26.330 --> 00:38:28.610
And then what I'm
going to show you

00:38:28.610 --> 00:38:32.690
is that this way
of computing things

00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:38.970
will make you read and write
less to the HBM, which is

00:38:38.970 --> 00:38:41.610
the whole point of this method.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:45.250
So let's see how
this looks like.

00:38:45.250 --> 00:38:49.310
Let's go back to our
self-attention computation,

00:38:49.310 --> 00:38:55.450
which, as a reminder, is
the softmax of q k transpose

00:38:55.450 --> 00:38:59.930
times v with some scaling
factor that we ignore right now.

00:38:59.930 --> 00:39:02.690
So here, what we're
saying is that we

00:39:02.690 --> 00:39:10.130
can compute the dot
product of q and k

00:39:10.130 --> 00:39:12.650
to obtain the s we had before.

00:39:12.650 --> 00:39:18.570
So here, s1, you can
think of it as q1 and k1.

00:39:18.570 --> 00:39:24.770
So what you can do is to
transfer these small elements

00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:30.650
to your SRAM, which
is small and fast.

00:39:30.650 --> 00:39:33.690
And you can transfer v1.

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:38.690
v1, which is also a small
quantity to your SRAM.

00:39:38.690 --> 00:39:43.690
And what you can do
is compute iteratively

00:39:43.690 --> 00:39:46.930
your numerator and
your denominator

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:52.710
for the weighted sum that
is corresponding to q1.

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:57.920
And then you repeat
that for the other keys.

00:39:57.920 --> 00:40:00.572
Because your query is
attending to the other keys,

00:40:00.572 --> 00:40:01.780
so you repeat the same thing.

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:04.360
So you're iterating on your sum.

00:40:04.360 --> 00:40:08.480
You're sending that
portion of the key

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:12.020
and that portion of the
value over to the SRAM.

00:40:12.020 --> 00:40:15.000
You are iterating on
these two quantities.

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:18.320
And you're doing
this again and again.

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:22.520
And what that means is
you're able to populate

00:40:22.520 --> 00:40:27.960
the results in your output
without having to materialize

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:31.800
your whole softmax matrix.

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:35.840
You don't need to store your
whole-- the huge n times n

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:37.840
matrix, you don't
have to do that.

00:40:37.840 --> 00:40:42.960
You can do that by sending small
components over to the SRAM,

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:49.680
do the computation, update the
numerator and the denominator.

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:53.150
And then at the end, just
have the two in order

00:40:53.150 --> 00:40:58.470
to obtain your desired results.

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:01.470
So you do that again and
again, and congrats, now you

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:09.070
have far fewer reads
and writes on your HBM.

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:12.350
Does that make sense?

00:41:12.350 --> 00:41:18.750
So I see a lot of places
that just present to you

00:41:18.750 --> 00:41:20.250
this whole formulas.

00:41:20.250 --> 00:41:24.630
And I just want to highlight
that these formulas illustrate

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:25.570
a few ideas.

00:41:25.570 --> 00:41:28.630
The first idea is that
you can iteratively

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:32.550
compute your numerator
and your denominator.

00:41:32.550 --> 00:41:35.030
And then the second
thing is we want

00:41:35.030 --> 00:41:39.270
to avoid numerical
instabilities by having

00:41:39.270 --> 00:41:44.070
an exponential of something
that is very big, which

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:47.870
is the reason why we're
doing this rescaling.

00:41:47.870 --> 00:41:51.310
And this is the reason why
we have these formulas, which

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:55.450
may seem scary, but it's
because of these two ideas.

00:41:58.510 --> 00:41:59.210
OK, cool.

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:01.590
So all of that is a
long way of saying

00:42:01.590 --> 00:42:07.830
that we are able to compute
the self-attention operation

00:42:07.830 --> 00:42:10.510
without having to read and
write to the HBM, which

00:42:10.510 --> 00:42:18.470
means that we're able to perform
that operation much faster.

00:42:18.470 --> 00:42:21.990
And so that is one thing.

00:42:21.990 --> 00:42:24.670
The second thing that I
want to highlight here

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:32.830
is, when you are training your
model, during the backward pass,

00:42:32.830 --> 00:42:36.550
you need to have
this matrix in order

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:40.110
to compute the
relevant quantity.

00:42:40.110 --> 00:42:42.590
Like when you do the
partial derivative

00:42:42.590 --> 00:42:46.950
of the loss with respect to
your parameter, this pops up.

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:52.520
And so before, if you want
to reuse these computations,

00:42:52.520 --> 00:42:56.860
you could store them in
the HBM, which is big.

00:42:56.860 --> 00:43:01.660
And you can read from it
during the backward pass.

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:06.240
But here, it turns out that
during the backward pass,

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:10.940
you actually can
afford to recompute

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:12.900
these quantities on the fly.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:16.220
Even though you had computed
it in the forward pass,

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:19.540
you can recompute them
in the backward pass

00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:30.260
and use that trick by using the
SRAM with this fused operation.

00:43:30.260 --> 00:43:33.700
And if you do that,
well, you actually

00:43:33.700 --> 00:43:36.460
have also some speedups.

00:43:36.460 --> 00:43:38.660
So I'm going to
state the obvious.

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:41.980
If you have computed
something, well, usually you

00:43:41.980 --> 00:43:45.180
don't have to compute it again,
otherwise, it's a waste of time.

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:48.870
But here, what we're saying
is when we compute something,

00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:51.610
if that something
is really big and we

00:43:51.610 --> 00:43:57.250
have to materialize it somewhere
that is slow to access,

00:43:57.250 --> 00:44:02.370
then it is sometimes worth it,
for instance, in this case,

00:44:02.370 --> 00:44:05.930
to just discard these
results and recompute

00:44:05.930 --> 00:44:13.330
that results on the fly again
to lead to these speedups.

00:44:13.330 --> 00:44:16.330
So in other words,
flash attention

00:44:16.330 --> 00:44:20.350
leads to more computations
at training time

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:21.890
because in the
backward pass, you're

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:26.370
not reusing the computations you
have done in the forward pass.

00:44:26.370 --> 00:44:29.890
You're actually recomputing
these quantities

00:44:29.890 --> 00:44:31.810
with this trick.

00:44:31.810 --> 00:44:36.410
So you have a higher compute,
higher number of gflops

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:37.870
that you can see on the table.

00:44:37.870 --> 00:44:40.710
So it's 75.2 versus 66.6.

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:42.290
By the way, these
numbers are just

00:44:42.290 --> 00:44:44.610
for your orders of magnitude.

00:44:44.610 --> 00:44:48.510
But the runtime
here is much less.

00:44:51.250 --> 00:44:52.730
So I hope that makes sense.

00:44:52.730 --> 00:44:54.810
So that is flash attention.

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:56.913
Yes?

00:44:56.913 --> 00:44:57.830
It's a great question.

00:44:57.830 --> 00:45:00.430
So the question is, is the
softmax that we computed,

00:45:00.430 --> 00:45:03.050
is it an approximation
of the original thing,

00:45:03.050 --> 00:45:05.430
or is it the actual thing?

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:08.490
And the answer is, it's
an exact computation.

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:11.470
You're not doing any
approximation whatsoever.

00:45:11.470 --> 00:45:16.050
What you're doing is
you're just sending

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:20.090
parts of what you want
to compute in a smart way

00:45:20.090 --> 00:45:24.570
in order to leverage the
asymmetry in the read

00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:27.730
and write time of some
components versus others.

00:45:27.730 --> 00:45:31.990
But here, if you compute the
numerator and the denominator--

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:33.750
and as we saw in
the very beginning,

00:45:33.750 --> 00:45:37.690
if you just divide
them and compare it

00:45:37.690 --> 00:45:40.410
to just your regular
formula, these

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:42.530
are exactly the same
formulas, but it's

00:45:42.530 --> 00:45:46.400
just that we're sending
them in a smarter way.

00:45:46.400 --> 00:45:51.260
It's a great question because
this, I want to emphasize,

00:45:51.260 --> 00:45:52.500
is an exact method.

00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:54.560
We're not
approximating anything.

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:58.200
We're just leveraging the
components of our GPUs

00:45:58.200 --> 00:46:01.020
in order to make these
operations just be faster.

00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:06.400
OK, great.

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:08.640
And I just want to
say, in the literature,

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:13.600
you may see different versions
of the flash attention methods.

00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:16.480
Flash attention 2,
3, 4, I just want

00:46:16.480 --> 00:46:21.200
to say that these papers, they
offer some optimization on top

00:46:21.200 --> 00:46:25.840
of what we saw, and they're also
being adapted to the new GPU

00:46:25.840 --> 00:46:27.267
versions.

00:46:27.267 --> 00:46:29.100
So yeah, they are also
a bit more technical.

00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:30.780
So feel free to read them.

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:33.120
We put some links on the slides.

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:36.720
But if there's something
that I want you to remember

00:46:36.720 --> 00:46:41.600
is this fused operation
trick that we saw

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:47.030
and the fact that we can also
recompute these quantities

00:46:47.030 --> 00:46:53.270
without storing them but still
have faster runtimes, which

00:46:53.270 --> 00:46:56.590
is not intuitively something
that would make sense.

00:46:56.590 --> 00:47:01.270
Because if you recompute
something, you would think

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:03.750
it takes more time,
but here it does not.

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:04.910
Yeah?

00:47:04.910 --> 00:47:06.970
So the question is, is
this only for training?

00:47:06.970 --> 00:47:11.670
Yes, the recomputation is for
training because here, you

00:47:11.670 --> 00:47:13.570
need to compute the gradient.

00:47:13.570 --> 00:47:15.950
So that's why you need
that for the first one.

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:18.630
You actually can do
that for forward pass.

00:47:18.630 --> 00:47:22.450
So you need to compute your
self-attention operation,

00:47:22.450 --> 00:47:25.973
and you can use
that first trick.

00:47:25.973 --> 00:47:26.890
It's a great question.

00:47:26.890 --> 00:47:29.057
The question is, well, if
you are at inference time,

00:47:29.057 --> 00:47:31.140
you don't need to do this
whole computation again.

00:47:31.140 --> 00:47:32.130
That's a great point.

00:47:32.130 --> 00:47:33.730
And Shervine will cover that.

00:47:36.590 --> 00:47:38.710
Yeah.

00:47:38.710 --> 00:47:42.910
So the question here is you
would use the same blocks

00:47:42.910 --> 00:47:45.310
in the vanilla case versus now.

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:46.630
So what is the difference?

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:47.650
Is that your question?

00:47:47.650 --> 00:47:48.450
OK, great.

00:47:48.450 --> 00:47:52.430
So the main difference
is, in the vanilla case,

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:55.510
in order for you
to be able to make

00:47:55.510 --> 00:47:59.630
the softmax of these
quantities times v operation,

00:47:59.630 --> 00:48:03.310
you would need to have
the sum of the components

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:07.710
through the whole
s matrix in store.

00:48:07.710 --> 00:48:09.110
You would need to have that.

00:48:09.110 --> 00:48:11.890
But here, you don't
need to have that.

00:48:11.890 --> 00:48:16.270
You can actually compute
that in an iterative fashion.

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:17.830
And so here, what
you need to do is

00:48:17.830 --> 00:48:20.550
to just update your
numerator and denominator

00:48:20.550 --> 00:48:22.590
and just keep on
going without having

00:48:22.590 --> 00:48:24.970
to wait to have the whole row.

00:48:24.970 --> 00:48:30.590
And as I mentioned, so the
matrix, that is this one,

00:48:30.590 --> 00:48:31.850
is upsized.

00:48:31.850 --> 00:48:34.870
So q and k, the
result is upsized

00:48:34.870 --> 00:48:37.630
n times n, which can be huge.

00:48:37.630 --> 00:48:39.080
And that's what
we want to avoid.

00:48:43.700 --> 00:48:44.200
OK, cool.

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:45.940
Yeah?

00:48:45.940 --> 00:48:48.300
So the question is,
what is the difference

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:50.160
between, for instance, 3, and 4?

00:48:50.160 --> 00:48:54.300
So in parentheses, there is the
name of the series of the GPU

00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:56.220
that is on the slide.

00:48:56.220 --> 00:49:00.700
So whenever you see
h, let's say 100, 200,

00:49:00.700 --> 00:49:03.360
it is actually part
of the Hopper series.

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:06.360
And when you see,
let's say, B100, B200,

00:49:06.360 --> 00:49:08.060
it's the Blackwell series.

00:49:08.060 --> 00:49:13.940
And these series, each
have some characteristics

00:49:13.940 --> 00:49:17.100
that are different from
one series to another.

00:49:17.100 --> 00:49:22.980
So these papers are just
meant to be even smarter

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:26.100
by taking the specificities
of these series

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:28.940
in into consideration.

00:49:28.940 --> 00:49:31.700
So it's a long way of
saying that it is a way

00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:35.420
to optimize this even more to
the specific type of GPU that

00:49:35.420 --> 00:49:38.030
is, for instance, in the
Hopper or the Blackwell series.

00:49:40.930 --> 00:49:41.750
OK, great.

00:49:41.750 --> 00:49:44.850
So this lecture is
meant to provide you

00:49:44.850 --> 00:49:49.530
with the methods that are
behind making all of this work

00:49:49.530 --> 00:49:51.250
without going too
much into the details.

00:49:51.250 --> 00:49:52.770
Because at the
end of the day, we

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:57.410
don't want to go too
much into the low level.

00:49:57.410 --> 00:50:00.350
That being said, flash attention
is a very important method.

00:50:00.350 --> 00:50:05.230
So in case you understand
the reason why we're doing it

00:50:05.230 --> 00:50:07.450
and how we're doing
it, then that's

00:50:07.450 --> 00:50:11.210
the main thing that,
I think, matters here.

00:50:11.210 --> 00:50:13.450
So going back to
the two questions

00:50:13.450 --> 00:50:16.210
that we asked at the very
beginning of the lecture, so

00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:18.290
how do we make this fit?

00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:21.690
So we saw here that
we were using a trick

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:27.290
to avoid having to store
the n by n attention matrix.

00:50:27.290 --> 00:50:29.890
So this is how we're
answering this question.

00:50:29.890 --> 00:50:32.210
The second one is, how
do we make it fast?

00:50:32.210 --> 00:50:36.050
And so here, we're
avoiding having

00:50:36.050 --> 00:50:40.570
to read and write from the HBM,
which is big but also slower

00:50:40.570 --> 00:50:42.250
than the SRAM.

00:50:42.250 --> 00:50:47.370
And that is how flash
attention is tackling this.

00:50:47.370 --> 00:50:48.170
Cool.

00:50:48.170 --> 00:50:48.670
OK.

00:50:48.670 --> 00:50:52.170
So now, we're moving on to
the second section, which

00:50:52.170 --> 00:50:56.010
is dedicated to training.

00:50:56.010 --> 00:50:59.590
And here, what we want to
do is to ask ourselves--

00:50:59.590 --> 00:51:01.630
OK, let's suppose
we have one GPU.

00:51:01.630 --> 00:51:04.050
Let's suppose we do
this flash attention.

00:51:04.050 --> 00:51:04.910
That's great.

00:51:04.910 --> 00:51:07.990
We can make things fit better.

00:51:07.990 --> 00:51:10.070
We can make things faster.

00:51:10.070 --> 00:51:13.370
But we may still
run out of memory

00:51:13.370 --> 00:51:15.670
because our model is too big.

00:51:15.670 --> 00:51:16.170
Cool.

00:51:16.170 --> 00:51:20.250
So here we're going to
tackle some methods for us

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:25.170
to be able to leverage
more than one GPU.

00:51:25.170 --> 00:51:29.770
And I want to just motivate
the need for this section

00:51:29.770 --> 00:51:35.320
by just saying that in order
for us to train LLMs, we take,

00:51:35.320 --> 00:51:38.380
as we mentioned, the
decoder of the transformer.

00:51:38.380 --> 00:51:39.480
We stack them up.

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:43.120
And then we're finding
just a bunch of tokens.

00:51:43.120 --> 00:51:47.800
And we just train that
model on a lot of data.

00:51:47.800 --> 00:51:51.960
And we'll see that we'll need
more than one GPU to do that.

00:51:51.960 --> 00:51:55.200
And I hope the back
of the napkin math

00:51:55.200 --> 00:51:59.480
that we did is something
that is convincing you.

00:51:59.480 --> 00:52:03.880
And here, I want to just
go over some reasons why

00:52:03.880 --> 00:52:09.440
the memory that we have on
one GPU can be insufficient.

00:52:09.440 --> 00:52:12.640
So one of the things that you
need to store on your memory

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:14.520
is, well, your model.

00:52:14.520 --> 00:52:17.080
Your model is on the order
of billions or trillions

00:52:17.080 --> 00:52:18.520
of parameters.

00:52:18.520 --> 00:52:21.180
And you need to
store that somewhere.

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:22.840
So that's one.

00:52:22.840 --> 00:52:25.480
The second thing is
you need to compute

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:26.745
the loss during training.

00:52:26.745 --> 00:52:28.120
And in order to
compute the loss,

00:52:28.120 --> 00:52:30.960
you need to do one forward pass.

00:52:30.960 --> 00:52:35.510
And so here, the
bigger your model,

00:52:35.510 --> 00:52:39.710
the bigger the amount of
activations you need to store.

00:52:39.710 --> 00:52:45.830
But it's also a function of the
sequence length and the batch

00:52:45.830 --> 00:52:46.710
size.

00:52:46.710 --> 00:52:49.950
So why sequence length?

00:52:49.950 --> 00:52:55.590
So we said that the transformer
is having all tokens interact

00:52:55.590 --> 00:52:57.990
with every other token.

00:52:57.990 --> 00:53:02.230
And so whenever you
process one single token,

00:53:02.230 --> 00:53:06.310
you compute activations that
are specific to that token.

00:53:06.310 --> 00:53:10.070
But you need to do that for
all other tokens as well.

00:53:10.070 --> 00:53:11.990
So this is the reason
why activations,

00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:16.310
they scale as a function
of your sequence length

00:53:16.310 --> 00:53:23.150
and, for obvious reasons, in
terms of the batch size as well.

00:53:23.150 --> 00:53:26.550
The other thing during
training is you need to learn

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:28.550
and you need to
update your weights.

00:53:28.550 --> 00:53:31.550
So you do a backward
pass in order

00:53:31.550 --> 00:53:36.510
to compute the direction that
will make your loss minimal,

00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:40.190
in other words, your gradient.

00:53:40.190 --> 00:53:44.750
And you need these
quantities as well.

00:53:44.750 --> 00:53:48.950
And you also need to potentially
store additional quantities.

00:53:48.950 --> 00:53:51.790
Because a lot of
the models nowadays,

00:53:51.790 --> 00:53:56.070
they're being trained with a
variant of the Adam optimizer.

00:53:56.070 --> 00:53:58.210
And just to illustrate,
in this case,

00:53:58.210 --> 00:54:01.710
the Adam optimizer
requires several quantities

00:54:01.710 --> 00:54:03.870
that you need to keep track of.

00:54:03.870 --> 00:54:09.590
And all these quantities also
need to be stored somewhere.

00:54:09.590 --> 00:54:14.190
So all of that to say that
the limited memory that we

00:54:14.190 --> 00:54:20.990
saw in the very first slides
is nowhere near enough.

00:54:20.990 --> 00:54:26.750
And this is the reason why
we need more than one GPU.

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:31.980
And I want to use that
as a motivation to talk

00:54:31.980 --> 00:54:34.908
about parallelism methods.

00:54:34.908 --> 00:54:36.700
The first method that
I want to talk to you

00:54:36.700 --> 00:54:41.020
about is called
data parallelism.

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:43.060
And the idea behind
data parallelism

00:54:43.060 --> 00:54:49.540
is when you want to compute your
forward and your backward pass,

00:54:49.540 --> 00:54:52.820
you don't have to do
that for all the data

00:54:52.820 --> 00:54:55.400
all at once in one device.

00:54:55.400 --> 00:55:02.300
You can very well divide your
data into multiple devices

00:55:02.300 --> 00:55:07.980
and then perform that
operation independently.

00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:11.100
So in a very naive
fashion, if you

00:55:11.100 --> 00:55:13.360
were to do that, you
could tell yourself,

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:19.620
well, let's replicate the
model on all these devices.

00:55:19.620 --> 00:55:23.660
So we would have the same
copy of parameters everywhere,

00:55:23.660 --> 00:55:28.330
copies of gradients everywhere,
copies of the optimizer state

00:55:28.330 --> 00:55:30.890
everywhere.

00:55:30.890 --> 00:55:36.050
But it's a lot of
redundant information.

00:55:36.050 --> 00:55:38.530
So this is the
reason why there's

00:55:38.530 --> 00:55:42.210
one method that is
particularly popular,

00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:47.810
which is called 0, which stands
for 0 redundancy optimizer,

00:55:47.810 --> 00:55:54.290
which actually shards these
quantities through your GPUs.

00:55:54.290 --> 00:55:57.070
So the idea is, well,
let's not repeat ourselves.

00:55:57.070 --> 00:55:59.690
Let's not store duplicates.

00:55:59.690 --> 00:56:03.430
And let's instead partition
them across our GPUs.

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:07.050
So you could do that
with the optimizer state.

00:56:07.050 --> 00:56:10.770
You could do that for
gradients, and you

00:56:10.770 --> 00:56:14.090
could do that for parameters.

00:56:14.090 --> 00:56:17.270
Well, it's not a free lunch
because if you want to perform,

00:56:17.270 --> 00:56:18.890
let's say, a forward
pass and if you

00:56:18.890 --> 00:56:22.730
don't have all your
parameters on your device,

00:56:22.730 --> 00:56:26.240
you need to have a way
to get those parameters.

00:56:26.240 --> 00:56:28.320
And so here, the way
you get those parameters

00:56:28.320 --> 00:56:33.080
is by communicating
with the other devices,

00:56:33.080 --> 00:56:37.000
performing some operations
to just retrieve those.

00:56:37.000 --> 00:56:40.200
And this adds some
communication costs.

00:56:40.200 --> 00:56:42.480
So it is not a free lunch.

00:56:42.480 --> 00:56:50.760
But it is allowing you to have
to store less memory per device.

00:56:50.760 --> 00:56:52.820
So that is one way.

00:56:52.820 --> 00:56:56.380
So data parallelism,
you divide your data.

00:56:56.380 --> 00:56:59.560
You distribute that
to several devices,

00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:02.377
and you perform
that independently.

00:57:02.377 --> 00:57:03.960
I mean, not independently
because then

00:57:03.960 --> 00:57:06.060
you need to synchronize
your gradient updates.

00:57:06.060 --> 00:57:08.200
So there is some
amount of communication

00:57:08.200 --> 00:57:12.240
that also is relevant
when it comes to when

00:57:12.240 --> 00:57:13.660
you shard your parameters.

00:57:16.200 --> 00:57:17.880
So that is one way.

00:57:17.880 --> 00:57:23.940
The other way is to
tell yourself, well,

00:57:23.940 --> 00:57:29.560
I don't want to just
split operations

00:57:29.560 --> 00:57:32.720
through the data dimension.

00:57:32.720 --> 00:57:36.040
I also want to leverage
different devices

00:57:36.040 --> 00:57:38.760
to perform model computations.

00:57:38.760 --> 00:57:43.160
So I want, let's say, device 1
to perform one part of the model

00:57:43.160 --> 00:57:43.820
computations.

00:57:43.820 --> 00:57:47.120
I want another part to be
performed on another device,

00:57:47.120 --> 00:57:49.360
and so on.

00:57:49.360 --> 00:57:56.360
So these methods are within the
category of model parallelism

00:57:56.360 --> 00:57:58.040
techniques.

00:57:58.040 --> 00:58:01.160
I'm just going to
mention a few of them.

00:58:01.160 --> 00:58:05.200
So the first one is
called tensor parallelism.

00:58:05.200 --> 00:58:06.640
Yes?

00:58:06.640 --> 00:58:11.200
So the question is, if you
shard your optimizer state,

00:58:11.200 --> 00:58:14.340
why is it not model parallelism?

00:58:14.340 --> 00:58:15.840
It's a great question.

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:20.360
So the key difference is
that, for model parallelism,

00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:24.070
you are performing
mean the computations

00:58:24.070 --> 00:58:29.230
for your model in a different
location, in a different device.

00:58:29.230 --> 00:58:33.470
But here, what we're saying
is that all the computations

00:58:33.470 --> 00:58:37.870
for a given subbatch of
data is on that device.

00:58:37.870 --> 00:58:42.510
And the way we're obtaining
the necessary information

00:58:42.510 --> 00:58:47.070
is by communicating
with the other devices.

00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:48.670
So I'll give you an example.

00:58:48.670 --> 00:58:50.950
Let's assume you have only
part of the parameters

00:58:50.950 --> 00:58:52.090
on your device.

00:58:52.090 --> 00:58:56.230
But you need to do the full,
let's say, forward pass.

00:58:56.230 --> 00:58:59.370
What you do is you
do your computations.

00:58:59.370 --> 00:59:02.870
And for the parameters that
you don't have on the device,

00:59:02.870 --> 00:59:06.390
you just ask your
other devices to send

00:59:06.390 --> 00:59:08.502
the necessary parameters.

00:59:08.502 --> 00:59:10.710
So you receive them, and
you perform the computations

00:59:10.710 --> 00:59:13.390
on that same device.

00:59:13.390 --> 00:59:17.230
And the same thing happens for
gradients and optimizer states.

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:21.740
And these two
quantities are actually

00:59:21.740 --> 00:59:23.780
associated with parameters.

00:59:23.780 --> 00:59:26.740
So what happens is, when
you shard the parameters

00:59:26.740 --> 00:59:30.300
across devices, along with
gradients and optimizer states,

00:59:30.300 --> 00:59:34.340
at the end of the day,
your GPU will only

00:59:34.340 --> 00:59:37.340
store the gradients
and optimizer

00:59:37.340 --> 00:59:42.700
states that are relevant to the
parameters that it has at hand.

00:59:42.700 --> 00:59:46.540
So long story short, this
is not model parallelism

00:59:46.540 --> 00:59:49.380
because we're performing
all the computations

00:59:49.380 --> 00:59:52.000
throughout the model
on that same device.

00:59:56.140 --> 00:59:58.300
Yeah?

00:59:58.300 --> 01:00:00.760
So the question
is, if we do this,

01:00:00.760 --> 01:00:03.160
don't we lose a lot of time
loading the parameters?

01:00:03.160 --> 01:00:04.360
So you have a great point.

01:00:04.360 --> 01:00:05.200
Yes, you do.

01:00:05.200 --> 01:00:08.400
But then you need to make
this run in the first place.

01:00:08.400 --> 01:00:13.260
So if you cannot fit what you
need to compute on one device,

01:00:13.260 --> 01:00:17.060
so you're choosing between not
being able to train it versus

01:00:17.060 --> 01:00:19.460
training it slowly.

01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:23.460
So yeah, the question is,
well, why is this one--

01:00:23.460 --> 01:00:26.640
how does this one compare
with tensor parallelism?

01:00:26.640 --> 01:00:28.500
And we're going to
see it right now.

01:00:28.500 --> 01:00:30.500
So tensor parallelism,
what we're saying

01:00:30.500 --> 01:00:34.700
is we're actually
splitting the computations

01:00:34.700 --> 01:00:37.020
across several devices.

01:00:37.020 --> 01:00:38.820
And in particular,
as we mentioned,

01:00:38.820 --> 01:00:40.980
there's a bunch of
matrix multiplications

01:00:40.980 --> 01:00:43.420
all over the place.

01:00:43.420 --> 01:00:46.020
And in the context
of self-attention,

01:00:46.020 --> 01:00:49.700
well, you have several heads.

01:00:49.700 --> 01:00:53.100
These computations can
be done independently.

01:00:53.100 --> 01:00:58.620
And so these, you can just
split it across devices.

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:01.540
In this figure, you do,
let's say, first device.

01:01:01.540 --> 01:01:04.260
Does the first head, the second
device does the second head.

01:01:04.260 --> 01:01:05.802
And then they each
do the projection,

01:01:05.802 --> 01:01:07.820
and then they get combined.

01:01:07.820 --> 01:01:11.980
So that one is an efficient
way of doing this because you

01:01:11.980 --> 01:01:15.460
can just independently do that.

01:01:15.460 --> 01:01:17.890
You can also do that
for other layers,

01:01:17.890 --> 01:01:21.130
for instance, the
feedforward neural network.

01:01:21.130 --> 01:01:22.890
And in particular,
here, if you see

01:01:22.890 --> 01:01:29.010
that matrix A can be decomposed
through the row, and then matrix

01:01:29.010 --> 01:01:32.670
B can be decomposed
across the y-axis.

01:01:32.670 --> 01:01:36.090
Then you can also
be smarter about it.

01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:38.930
I want to answer your
question directly.

01:01:38.930 --> 01:01:41.770
It is not a question
of either/or.

01:01:41.770 --> 01:01:43.730
It's not either you
do data parallelism

01:01:43.730 --> 01:01:45.550
or you do tensor parallelism.

01:01:45.550 --> 01:01:47.890
You can actually
combine the methods.

01:01:47.890 --> 01:01:50.570
So nothing is stopping
you from doing

01:01:50.570 --> 01:01:54.610
this method, along with the
data parallelism methods,

01:01:54.610 --> 01:02:00.010
if you feel like it's not
going to fit in your memory.

01:02:00.010 --> 01:02:04.090
So this is tensor parallelism
is the first kind of model

01:02:04.090 --> 01:02:05.543
parallelism technique.

01:02:05.543 --> 01:02:07.210
The second one that
I want to talk about

01:02:07.210 --> 01:02:10.690
is called pipeline parallelism.

01:02:10.690 --> 01:02:17.240
And here, what we're saying is
that if we draw the whole model

01:02:17.240 --> 01:02:19.960
computation as,
let's say, a sequence

01:02:19.960 --> 01:02:22.720
of different components-- so
you have, let's say, component

01:02:22.720 --> 01:02:25.560
0, component 1, component 2,
component 3, what we're saying

01:02:25.560 --> 01:02:31.220
is the first part of the
computation is on one device.

01:02:31.220 --> 01:02:35.320
The second part is on
another device, and so on.

01:02:35.320 --> 01:02:39.200
So this allows us to
split the computations

01:02:39.200 --> 01:02:44.120
through across the layers.

01:02:44.120 --> 01:02:49.400
And so here, I just want you
to pay attention to the arrows.

01:02:49.400 --> 01:02:54.880
So here, the first device will
be sending the information

01:02:54.880 --> 01:02:58.520
to the second device
in the forward pass.

01:02:58.520 --> 01:03:01.680
But it will also
keep some information

01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:03.820
for the backward
pass for that stage.

01:03:03.820 --> 01:03:06.900
Because in the backward pass,
you're computing the gradients.

01:03:06.900 --> 01:03:08.520
And when you compute
the gradients,

01:03:08.520 --> 01:03:11.640
you have the activation
that is required to perform

01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:15.240
the gradient operation.

01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:22.080
The problem when you do that
is that your GPUs can be idle.

01:03:22.080 --> 01:03:24.440
They can be just sitting
and doing nothing,

01:03:24.440 --> 01:03:29.600
and you will see this phenomenon
being branded as bubble.

01:03:29.600 --> 01:03:33.360
So if you have your, let's say,
device 0 doing the forward pass

01:03:33.360 --> 01:03:39.320
and then passing it to
device 1, your device 0

01:03:39.320 --> 01:03:43.440
will be waiting up until
it is its turn in order

01:03:43.440 --> 01:03:45.680
to do the backward pass.

01:03:45.680 --> 01:03:49.320
So people, they come up
with a bunch of strategies

01:03:49.320 --> 01:03:51.760
to minimize that idle time.

01:03:51.760 --> 01:03:54.360
We will not cover this
here, but just want

01:03:54.360 --> 01:03:57.440
to say that this is
an area that people

01:03:57.440 --> 01:04:01.880
are working towards optimizing.

01:04:01.880 --> 01:04:03.940
So we've seen
tensor parallelism.

01:04:03.940 --> 01:04:08.260
So we divide operations
at the matrix level.

01:04:08.260 --> 01:04:12.150
Pipeline parallelism, it's
across the whole layers.

01:04:12.150 --> 01:04:16.750
Third one is
context parallelism.

01:04:16.750 --> 01:04:22.710
That one is with respect
to the sequence length.

01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:26.470
Because if you remember,
when we do self-attention,

01:04:26.470 --> 01:04:31.030
we do one token attends
to every other token.

01:04:31.030 --> 01:04:33.970
But sometimes your sequence
length is very big,

01:04:33.970 --> 01:04:38.830
so you need to compute
queries and keys and values

01:04:38.830 --> 01:04:42.270
for everything and just
do a bunch of operations.

01:04:42.270 --> 01:04:47.470
So here, what we're
proposing is to reserve

01:04:47.470 --> 01:04:54.230
groups of queries per device and
then move the keys and values

01:04:54.230 --> 01:04:55.990
around.

01:04:55.990 --> 01:04:57.290
So I'll give you an example.

01:04:57.290 --> 01:05:02.270
Let's say I have two devices
and I have two tokens.

01:05:02.270 --> 01:05:05.230
The first token is on device 1.

01:05:05.230 --> 01:05:08.150
The second token is on device 2.

01:05:08.150 --> 01:05:12.260
So an idea is to say, OK,
device 1 does query 1.

01:05:12.260 --> 01:05:15.660
Device 2 does query 2.

01:05:15.660 --> 01:05:20.740
Device 1 computes
key 1 and value 1.

01:05:20.740 --> 01:05:26.100
Device 2 computes
key 2 and value 2.

01:05:26.100 --> 01:05:32.660
Device 1 and device 2 do
the operation of the query 1

01:05:32.660 --> 01:05:39.140
with k1, v1, query
2, and k2, v2.

01:05:39.140 --> 01:05:44.100
And when they're done, they
just give their keys and values

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:46.500
to the other device.

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:47.640
And they repeat that.

01:05:47.640 --> 01:05:50.640
So I mentioned this
example for two elements.

01:05:50.640 --> 01:05:54.700
But just imagine for a
bigger number of elements,

01:05:54.700 --> 01:06:02.100
you can actually divide this
operation per groups of tokens

01:06:02.100 --> 01:06:05.100
and just give the keys and
values to your neighbor

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:08.020
to do that again.

01:06:08.020 --> 01:06:10.340
That is context parallelism.

01:06:10.340 --> 01:06:14.320
And then you have a fifth one.

01:06:14.320 --> 01:06:16.240
So it's a fourth for
model parallelism.

01:06:16.240 --> 01:06:19.540
But on top of data parallelism
is the fifth type of parallelism

01:06:19.540 --> 01:06:23.540
that we see, expert parallelism.

01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:30.500
So let me remind you that we saw
that as we scale large language

01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:34.700
models, we may not want
to use all the parameters

01:06:34.700 --> 01:06:35.840
during a forward pass.

01:06:35.840 --> 01:06:39.460
And we may introduce
the concept of experts

01:06:39.460 --> 01:06:43.180
at the level of feedforward
neural networks.

01:06:43.180 --> 01:06:48.540
And here, what we're saying
is when we do our model

01:06:48.540 --> 01:06:51.460
computation, we're
actually deciding

01:06:51.460 --> 01:06:56.260
on which expert to send
our token to and only

01:06:56.260 --> 01:07:00.340
performing computation
on that expert.

01:07:00.340 --> 01:07:06.540
So here, an idea
is to say that we

01:07:06.540 --> 01:07:10.410
put our experts on
different devices,

01:07:10.410 --> 01:07:13.930
and we start, let's
say, with a random way

01:07:13.930 --> 01:07:16.570
of assigning our tokens
to different devices.

01:07:16.570 --> 01:07:19.250
We perform the
self-attention operation.

01:07:19.250 --> 01:07:23.050
And when it comes to going into
the feedforward neural network

01:07:23.050 --> 01:07:31.170
part, we figure out where
each token should go to.

01:07:31.170 --> 01:07:34.330
And then we send that
token on the device

01:07:34.330 --> 01:07:39.930
that has the expert that
the token was assigned to.

01:07:39.930 --> 01:07:44.210
And then it does that, and then
it comes back to the device,

01:07:44.210 --> 01:07:47.290
and so on.

01:07:47.290 --> 01:07:49.130
So all of that to
say-- so I hope I

01:07:49.130 --> 01:07:51.810
didn't overwhelm you with
all these different types

01:07:51.810 --> 01:07:52.710
of parallelism.

01:07:52.710 --> 01:07:57.770
All of that to say that if
you have more than one GPU,

01:07:57.770 --> 01:08:03.490
you can find ways to
divide your computations

01:08:03.490 --> 01:08:08.040
either from a data perspective
or from a model computation

01:08:08.040 --> 01:08:09.960
perspective.

01:08:09.960 --> 01:08:14.720
And we saw five different
types of parallelisms.

01:08:14.720 --> 01:08:18.880
And you will see out there
the five D's, five dimension

01:08:18.880 --> 01:08:23.720
of parallelism,
data parallelism.

01:08:23.720 --> 01:08:25.560
Let me see.

01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:27.479
Tensor parallelism.

01:08:27.479 --> 01:08:29.040
Pipeline parallelism.

01:08:29.040 --> 01:08:33.240
Context parallelism
and parallelism.

01:08:33.240 --> 01:08:37.160
Nothing prevents you from
mixing some form of parallelism

01:08:37.160 --> 01:08:38.960
with another one.

01:08:38.960 --> 01:08:43.560
The reason why you would
not want to parallelize this

01:08:43.560 --> 01:08:48.500
to the max is because, well,
if you parallelize this,

01:08:48.500 --> 01:08:51.640
you are trading that
off with your devices

01:08:51.640 --> 01:08:54.200
not being fully utilized.

01:08:54.200 --> 01:08:58.600
And you may or may not know,
but GPUs are very expensive.

01:08:58.600 --> 01:09:04.120
And people rent GPUs with
some price tag per hour.

01:09:04.120 --> 01:09:07.160
And then I think you
mentioned that very clearly,

01:09:07.160 --> 01:09:10.000
you also incur some
communication costs.

01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:14.720
Because in some cases, for
instance, the 0, 3 data

01:09:14.720 --> 01:09:18.880
parallelism variant, you need
to communicate the parameters

01:09:18.880 --> 01:09:21.460
in order to be able to
perform your computation.

01:09:21.460 --> 01:09:24.720
So it's not a free lunch either.

01:09:24.720 --> 01:09:28.520
Just to summarize,
how do we make it fit?

01:09:28.520 --> 01:09:32.580
Well, we distribute parameters,
activations, gradients,

01:09:32.580 --> 01:09:34.080
states across GPUs.

01:09:34.080 --> 01:09:36.840
And how do we make it fast?

01:09:36.840 --> 01:09:40.120
Well, we can process these
different batches in parallel

01:09:40.120 --> 01:09:41.920
with data parallelism
techniques.

01:09:41.920 --> 01:09:45.040
And we can also parallelize
model computations

01:09:45.040 --> 01:09:49.960
with model parallelism
techniques.

01:09:49.960 --> 01:09:54.240
And with that, I'll
give it to Shervine.

01:09:54.240 --> 01:09:55.840
Thank you, Afshine.

01:09:55.840 --> 01:09:59.200
So now, I want us to
build some intuition

01:09:59.200 --> 01:10:01.960
for what happens when it
comes to optimizations

01:10:01.960 --> 01:10:03.990
at inference time.

01:10:03.990 --> 01:10:06.790
So I'm going to
start with two cases,

01:10:06.790 --> 01:10:08.790
and I'm going to need your help.

01:10:08.790 --> 01:10:15.350
So let's suppose in one case, I
have a 10-token query and I ask

01:10:15.350 --> 01:10:19.710
for a 10k-token output.

01:10:19.710 --> 01:10:24.630
So again, generate a story for
my teddy bear to fall asleep.

01:10:24.630 --> 01:10:26.910
And the second one
being opposites,

01:10:26.910 --> 01:10:33.350
so I have a 10k-token input
and a 10-token output.

01:10:33.350 --> 01:10:36.510
So which one is faster
in your opinion?

01:10:39.070 --> 01:10:39.930
The second one?

01:10:39.930 --> 01:10:40.430
Why?

01:10:46.350 --> 01:10:48.650
So the answer is you don't
have to compute the loss.

01:10:48.650 --> 01:10:51.590
So we're not at
training time here.

01:10:51.590 --> 01:10:54.330
But this is the good path.

01:10:54.330 --> 01:10:56.790
Yeah?

01:10:56.790 --> 01:11:00.150
So the answer here is
that in the second one,

01:11:00.150 --> 01:11:02.700
you need to do
autoregressive decoding one

01:11:02.700 --> 01:11:06.000
at a time versus everything
in parallel at first.

01:11:06.000 --> 01:11:07.800
So that's a great answer.

01:11:07.800 --> 01:11:10.180
Is that the same that
you wanted to say?

01:11:10.180 --> 01:11:12.380
So this is great.

01:11:12.380 --> 01:11:16.240
So your intuition can be written
down in terms of an equation.

01:11:16.240 --> 01:11:20.580
And we're going to see which one
contributed here to the latency.

01:11:20.580 --> 01:11:23.660
So when you issue
a request, there

01:11:23.660 --> 01:11:26.820
is a time that we
have not talked

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:30.820
so far about, which is Tq.

01:11:30.820 --> 01:11:34.020
So of course, your LLM
provider has more than just you

01:11:34.020 --> 01:11:34.800
as a user.

01:11:34.800 --> 01:11:39.180
So you have your query
but others have this.

01:11:39.180 --> 01:11:41.620
This is part of
batching strategies

01:11:41.620 --> 01:11:43.400
that we're not going
to cover today,

01:11:43.400 --> 01:11:46.700
but they do influence
the latency that you're

01:11:46.700 --> 01:11:48.660
going to see as a user.

01:11:48.660 --> 01:11:53.940
But then you have a time
to compute all these keys

01:11:53.940 --> 01:11:58.300
and values that you mentioned
well, which is T prefill.

01:11:58.300 --> 01:12:01.980
And since you're doing this
operation in a teacher forcing

01:12:01.980 --> 01:12:05.780
way, meaning that you put all
your tokens of your prompts

01:12:05.780 --> 01:12:08.620
as input at the same
time, this is typically

01:12:08.620 --> 01:12:12.380
fast relatively to the
number of tokens at stake.

01:12:12.380 --> 01:12:16.940
And on the other hand,
this autoregression concept

01:12:16.940 --> 01:12:20.420
is what takes you a lot of time.

01:12:20.420 --> 01:12:24.260
So here, I'm going to
introduce some variables to put

01:12:24.260 --> 01:12:27.200
some numbers into all
of these phenomenons.

01:12:27.200 --> 01:12:37.540
So TTFT, time to first token,
and TPOT, time per output token,

01:12:37.540 --> 01:12:44.060
and TTLT, time to last token.

01:12:44.060 --> 01:12:46.620
So now, if you are
an infra person,

01:12:46.620 --> 01:12:50.940
you're going to be interested
in monitoring the two first ones

01:12:50.940 --> 01:12:52.780
quite closely because
you want to know

01:12:52.780 --> 01:12:56.980
whether your queuing strategy
and your prefill strategy,

01:12:56.980 --> 01:13:01.270
like how you parallelize
all these input tokens,

01:13:01.270 --> 01:13:03.970
you want to know
whether that works well.

01:13:03.970 --> 01:13:06.650
Also, you want to make sure
that your time per input

01:13:06.650 --> 01:13:12.250
token per output token is good
because you want your user

01:13:12.250 --> 01:13:17.490
to see the outputs being
generated in a convenient way.

01:13:17.490 --> 01:13:23.390
So you will see also TTLT being
reported in the infra world,

01:13:23.390 --> 01:13:28.010
but this matters a bit less
from a pure infra perspective,

01:13:28.010 --> 01:13:32.690
because one could argue that
TTLT is intrinsically linked

01:13:32.690 --> 01:13:34.810
to what your model does.

01:13:34.810 --> 01:13:38.010
So let's say it does
a bunch of reasoning.

01:13:38.010 --> 01:13:41.370
Then everything that's
in the reasoning parts

01:13:41.370 --> 01:13:45.010
will artificially increase
the time to last token.

01:13:45.010 --> 01:13:49.330
Let's say your users issue a
lot of queries about asking

01:13:49.330 --> 01:13:51.570
for teddy bear stories.

01:13:51.570 --> 01:13:54.810
That will increase the TTLT
but not necessarily mean

01:13:54.810 --> 01:13:56.720
that your system is bad.

01:13:56.720 --> 01:13:59.480
So the two first
ones are typically

01:13:59.480 --> 01:14:02.760
what you want to minimize.

01:14:02.760 --> 01:14:05.920
And yeah, there is
a question here.

01:14:05.920 --> 01:14:08.460
So the comment here,
what are you saying?

01:14:08.460 --> 01:14:11.640
Why is the per
output token set slow

01:14:11.640 --> 01:14:12.940
whereas prefilled is set fast?

01:14:12.940 --> 01:14:15.560
So it's relative to
what you would expect.

01:14:15.560 --> 01:14:17.760
So you're right that the
per output token might

01:14:17.760 --> 01:14:19.880
be faster than the T prefilled.

01:14:19.880 --> 01:14:23.780
But I just want to give an
overview of why this is fast.

01:14:23.780 --> 01:14:26.800
Typically, if you ask
several questions to your LLM

01:14:26.800 --> 01:14:31.100
or if you go and
issue further prompts,

01:14:31.100 --> 01:14:34.440
you have all these tokens
that are pre-computed,

01:14:34.440 --> 01:14:37.040
and you have this
concept of also caching

01:14:37.040 --> 01:14:39.960
of your past interactions
or caching of the system

01:14:39.960 --> 01:14:43.440
prompt that will
come into the picture

01:14:43.440 --> 01:14:46.360
and that will contribute
into you not seeing

01:14:46.360 --> 01:14:49.420
the time for prefilling
all this to be that long.

01:14:49.420 --> 01:14:53.900
So the short story is that
it's all relative to per token.

01:14:58.025 --> 01:15:01.160
Does that make sense?

01:15:01.160 --> 01:15:03.380
Awesome.

01:15:03.380 --> 01:15:07.400
So now, let's take a
look at some prices just

01:15:07.400 --> 01:15:10.880
to hone our intuition.

01:15:10.880 --> 01:15:17.000
So here, you see some big labs
with input prices and output

01:15:17.000 --> 01:15:17.580
prices.

01:15:17.580 --> 01:15:22.600
So you see that the output price
is empirically five times more

01:15:22.600 --> 01:15:23.660
than input prices.

01:15:23.660 --> 01:15:26.800
So there is no ground
truth relationship that

01:15:26.800 --> 01:15:28.180
dictates it should be five.

01:15:28.180 --> 01:15:31.120
But it's interesting
to note that.

01:15:31.120 --> 01:15:36.440
And we will motivate why this is
an expensive computation later

01:15:36.440 --> 01:15:36.940
on.

01:15:36.940 --> 01:15:43.240
But I feel you have already
gotten a gist of why.

01:15:43.240 --> 01:15:46.360
And when you are
managing an LLM,

01:15:46.360 --> 01:15:48.520
you're interested
in several things.

01:15:48.520 --> 01:15:55.830
So two of them are the focus of
today, which are fast and cheap.

01:15:55.830 --> 01:15:59.630
And when we say fast
and cheap, I just

01:15:59.630 --> 01:16:01.990
want to put some
quantitative number.

01:16:01.990 --> 01:16:05.510
Maybe you have some
requirements as a system owner.

01:16:05.510 --> 01:16:08.170
So maybe tau 1,
tau 2 requirements.

01:16:08.170 --> 01:16:12.230
And you want for your
times per first token

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:17.790
or time per output token to
be within this upper bound.

01:16:17.790 --> 01:16:22.670
Same for the cost, so maybe you
have some quantitative number

01:16:22.670 --> 01:16:26.110
that you want to
constrain this on.

01:16:26.110 --> 01:16:30.950
And now, I want to
introduce some concepts that

01:16:30.950 --> 01:16:34.270
can help us gauge where
we are with respect

01:16:34.270 --> 01:16:40.030
to the memory versus compute
game, because both of them

01:16:40.030 --> 01:16:44.110
compete for not
being the bottleneck.

01:16:44.110 --> 01:16:47.490
So first, let's talk about
computational throughput.

01:16:47.490 --> 01:16:51.900
So when we take the specs sheet
that Afshine had mentioned,

01:16:51.900 --> 01:16:55.740
it's all these teraflops
numbers that you see there.

01:16:55.740 --> 01:16:57.800
So this is a function
of your data type.

01:16:57.800 --> 01:17:02.840
So of course, if your numbers
are more likely represented,

01:17:02.840 --> 01:17:07.540
it will be able to perform
more operations per second.

01:17:07.540 --> 01:17:09.720
And then you have
a memory bandwidth,

01:17:09.720 --> 01:17:14.220
which is bottlenecked by how
fast you can transfer data

01:17:14.220 --> 01:17:18.220
from the HBM to the SRAM
towards computation.

01:17:18.220 --> 01:17:22.140
So here, we had seen an order
of magnitude in the past.

01:17:22.140 --> 01:17:25.900
And now, I want to
introduce a concept that

01:17:25.900 --> 01:17:30.600
is going to be of interest for
us, called arithmetic intensity.

01:17:30.600 --> 01:17:36.460
That will link compute
speed with memory moved.

01:17:36.460 --> 01:17:39.940
So it's defined by the
ratio of computations

01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:42.740
done over memory moves.

01:17:42.740 --> 01:17:48.020
And we're going to see very
soon how they relate to others.

01:17:48.020 --> 01:17:50.010
So I want to take a
pause and make sure

01:17:50.010 --> 01:17:55.050
that these three definitions
make sense to everyone.

01:17:55.050 --> 01:17:55.630
Yeah?

01:17:58.510 --> 01:18:01.270
So the question is, which
one is easiest to achieve?

01:18:01.270 --> 01:18:05.610
So these are all given
by your hardware.

01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:09.850
So you'll have some number
that come out at the end.

01:18:09.850 --> 01:18:13.210
And depending on your workflow,
we're going to see in a second

01:18:13.210 --> 01:18:17.570
that we're going to be faced
with either too slow of a memory

01:18:17.570 --> 01:18:20.050
or too slow of computations.

01:18:20.050 --> 01:18:22.930
And I think this
is going to define

01:18:22.930 --> 01:18:24.270
the trade-off between the two.

01:18:24.270 --> 01:18:28.650
But as a user, I mean,
you should take them as

01:18:28.650 --> 01:18:29.590
given to you.

01:18:29.590 --> 01:18:32.880
And I don't see any sense
of relative difficulty.

01:18:32.880 --> 01:18:34.630
But I think they all
have their challenge,

01:18:34.630 --> 01:18:37.490
if you were to ask
a hardware person.

01:18:37.490 --> 01:18:41.290
Yeah OK.

01:18:41.290 --> 01:18:47.650
So now I'm going to zoom
in to Afshine's GPU schema,

01:18:47.650 --> 01:18:50.450
and then we're going to put
these orders of magnitude

01:18:50.450 --> 01:18:52.782
on there just to
make sure we are

01:18:52.782 --> 01:18:53.990
talking about the same thing.

01:18:53.990 --> 01:19:00.330
So the HBM, we saw its order
of magnitude of 100 gigabytes.

01:19:00.330 --> 01:19:05.610
The SRAM is much
less, 100 megabytes.

01:19:05.610 --> 01:19:09.330
And we said that
the memory bandwidth

01:19:09.330 --> 01:19:13.810
was in the order of magnitude
of terabytes per second.

01:19:13.810 --> 01:19:17.270
And then compute units,
thousands of teraflops.

01:19:17.270 --> 01:19:20.230
But as we saw, it's highly
dependent on your data type.

01:19:20.230 --> 01:19:23.350
So do not quote me on
this without details.

01:19:23.350 --> 01:19:25.090
Yeah?

01:19:25.090 --> 01:19:29.210
So the question is, how come
you have a TB versus MB?

01:19:29.210 --> 01:19:32.650
So it means that you are
going to transfer things

01:19:32.650 --> 01:19:35.770
in below a second
because per second

01:19:35.770 --> 01:19:38.850
is a measure-- a unit of time.

01:19:38.850 --> 01:19:39.530
Yeah.

01:19:39.530 --> 01:19:40.990
But I mean, great point.

01:19:40.990 --> 01:19:43.050
It's happening quite fast.

01:19:43.050 --> 01:19:45.250
That's the intuition here.

01:19:45.250 --> 01:19:46.560
Yeah?

01:19:46.560 --> 01:19:49.280
So the question here
is you're talking

01:19:49.280 --> 01:19:53.717
to me about memory bandwidth
being some bottleneck.

01:19:53.717 --> 01:19:55.300
Why don't you just
make it run faster?

01:19:55.300 --> 01:19:56.758
So if you're a
hardware person, you

01:19:56.758 --> 01:20:00.180
will know that these,
when you print them,

01:20:00.180 --> 01:20:01.780
they are much bigger in size.

01:20:01.780 --> 01:20:06.640
So you have some trade-off to
make when you design your GPU.

01:20:06.640 --> 01:20:10.360
So yeah, HBM is much bigger
and also much smaller

01:20:10.360 --> 01:20:11.220
to put in place.

01:20:11.220 --> 01:20:15.200
So you have some trade-off here.

01:20:15.200 --> 01:20:18.900
But in a perfect world, you will
have the biggest SRAM possible.

01:20:18.900 --> 01:20:24.720
You have the most CU, compute
units, power possible.

01:20:24.720 --> 01:20:26.700
But yeah, sadly, not possible.

01:20:29.680 --> 01:20:31.040
OK, awesome.

01:20:31.040 --> 01:20:33.800
So now, I'm going to talk to
you about the relationship

01:20:33.800 --> 01:20:36.820
between two of those
dimensions that we mentioned.

01:20:36.820 --> 01:20:39.400
So one, arithmetic intensity.

01:20:39.400 --> 01:20:42.280
That shows the
amount of compute you

01:20:42.280 --> 01:20:45.950
can get done per
memory moved and that

01:20:45.950 --> 01:20:48.730
as a function of
computational throughput.

01:20:48.730 --> 01:20:51.230
So how many
operations per second?

01:20:51.230 --> 01:20:54.630
And when you draw the
relationship between the two,

01:20:54.630 --> 01:21:00.110
you see that there is an upper
bound to it, which is this line.

01:21:00.110 --> 01:21:05.270
And we call it the
roofline model.

01:21:05.270 --> 01:21:07.970
Has anyone heard of the
roofline model so far?

01:21:07.970 --> 01:21:09.110
Yeah, a few people.

01:21:09.110 --> 01:21:11.830
So it's called roofline
model because the line just

01:21:11.830 --> 01:21:13.030
looks like a roof.

01:21:13.030 --> 01:21:16.230
There is no deeper
meaning to it.

01:21:16.230 --> 01:21:19.390
And the breaking point that
you see between the two

01:21:19.390 --> 01:21:21.590
is called the ridge point.

01:21:21.590 --> 01:21:24.950
And it's the point
at which you are

01:21:24.950 --> 01:21:31.190
being neither memory-bound
nor compute-bound.

01:21:31.190 --> 01:21:33.310
And just to give
some intuition as

01:21:33.310 --> 01:21:35.910
to what is going to
be of interest to us

01:21:35.910 --> 01:21:42.230
at inference time, so as the
intuition was said previously,

01:21:42.230 --> 01:21:46.390
you have a lot of memory
with very few computes

01:21:46.390 --> 01:21:48.030
at inference time
because you load

01:21:48.030 --> 01:21:51.190
all the weights of
your model, and you

01:21:51.190 --> 01:21:54.170
try to predict just
one token at a time.

01:21:54.170 --> 01:21:56.970
So you're going to be
in the purple section.

01:21:56.970 --> 01:22:01.270
When you're at decode time,
you're going to be memory-bound.

01:22:01.270 --> 01:22:06.630
And when you do smart tricks
like the one Afshine mentioned,

01:22:06.630 --> 01:22:11.210
like flash attention, if you do
your job well, at prefill time,

01:22:11.210 --> 01:22:14.390
you should be in a
compute-bound region.

01:22:14.390 --> 01:22:17.570
Because you are optimized
in terms of memory,

01:22:17.570 --> 01:22:20.530
you have a lot of computations
to do in parallel.

01:22:20.530 --> 01:22:25.870
So this should be
your bottleneck.

01:22:25.870 --> 01:22:28.330
And to be
mathematically precise,

01:22:28.330 --> 01:22:34.590
you can even draw a relationship
at this reach point.

01:22:34.590 --> 01:22:36.670
So did that make sense so far?

01:22:36.670 --> 01:22:39.790
Yeah, the question is, why did
the graph start from non-zero?

01:22:39.790 --> 01:22:40.730
It's my bad.

01:22:40.730 --> 01:22:43.120
So usually, these graphs,
they are on a log scale.

01:22:43.120 --> 01:22:46.400
So when you zoom in, it doesn't
go to 0, but it should go to 0.

01:22:46.400 --> 01:22:50.020
Yeah, great point, because
it's a linear relationship.

01:22:50.020 --> 01:22:51.280
And awesomely said.

01:22:51.280 --> 01:22:54.520
So now, I'm going to go
quickly through a KV caching.

01:22:54.520 --> 01:22:57.740
So we have all seen that when
we are at inference time,

01:22:57.740 --> 01:23:05.200
we reuse the keys and values
that were computed in the past.

01:23:05.200 --> 01:23:08.700
So this is why we're interested
in maintaining a cache.

01:23:08.700 --> 01:23:13.520
So let's say you want to
compute a cute teddy bear.

01:23:13.520 --> 01:23:15.860
So let's say you have--

01:23:15.860 --> 01:23:18.220
then the keys and
values will be reused

01:23:18.220 --> 01:23:25.820
later on for your later tokens
to attend to it, and so on.

01:23:25.820 --> 01:23:29.580
And when it comes to
caching, since you

01:23:29.580 --> 01:23:32.540
reuse existing
computations, this

01:23:32.540 --> 01:23:35.100
is typically going
to be much cheaper.

01:23:35.100 --> 01:23:38.220
So just to take one
of these graphs,

01:23:38.220 --> 01:23:43.130
if you have a cached input,
then it's going to be about 1/10

01:23:43.130 --> 01:23:47.450
compared to if you were to give
new inputs to the provider.

01:23:47.450 --> 01:23:51.210
And I want to do a
second caveat here.

01:23:51.210 --> 01:23:54.930
The caching that we're talking
here is at decode time,

01:23:54.930 --> 01:23:57.250
and you're computing
each token at a time

01:23:57.250 --> 01:23:59.370
by relying on some KV cache.

01:23:59.370 --> 01:24:04.090
The pricing of that caching is
included in the output cost.

01:24:04.090 --> 01:24:05.810
And the caching that
I'm mentioning here

01:24:05.810 --> 01:24:09.430
is typically one where
you compare turns.

01:24:09.430 --> 01:24:13.650
So let's say you have had a
turn and you issue a new query.

01:24:13.650 --> 01:24:15.850
Then that query
will anchor on what

01:24:15.850 --> 01:24:18.370
you have discussed in
the past, and then those

01:24:18.370 --> 01:24:19.990
would be part of your cache.

01:24:19.990 --> 01:24:22.070
So this is not new input.

01:24:22.070 --> 01:24:25.430
These are states already
computed by your provider.

01:24:25.430 --> 01:24:30.010
And these are the
prices that you see.

01:24:30.010 --> 01:24:32.850
OK, awesome.

01:24:32.850 --> 01:24:34.610
Yeah?

01:24:34.610 --> 01:24:37.310
So the question here,
what about causal masking?

01:24:37.310 --> 01:24:39.930
So there is a trick where
you multiply all of these

01:24:39.930 --> 01:24:42.410
by some triangular matrix.

01:24:42.410 --> 01:24:44.470
So suppose it's
all taken care of.

01:24:49.650 --> 01:24:52.730
So now, I'm going
to move on to--

01:24:52.730 --> 01:24:54.910
let's say you are an
inference provider.

01:24:54.910 --> 01:24:58.530
You have your GPU
and a bunch of memory

01:24:58.530 --> 01:25:04.810
that's allocatable to KV
cache, like keys and values

01:25:04.810 --> 01:25:09.130
and you want to see how many
queries you can accommodate.

01:25:09.130 --> 01:25:11.770
So let's say you issue a prompt.

01:25:11.770 --> 01:25:14.770
All of these providers,
they will tell you,

01:25:14.770 --> 01:25:21.450
hey, you can have a
maximum of x output.

01:25:21.450 --> 01:25:23.490
So there is nothing
that prevents

01:25:23.490 --> 01:25:27.250
when you see an input
query from expecting

01:25:27.250 --> 01:25:29.930
that size as an output.

01:25:29.930 --> 01:25:34.190
So if you were to naively
design memory management,

01:25:34.190 --> 01:25:40.120
you would need to reserve all
of that as a block in your GPU.

01:25:40.120 --> 01:25:45.400
But the issue is, when you come
with your actual completion,

01:25:45.400 --> 01:25:48.340
it's often much less
than the maximum output.

01:25:48.340 --> 01:25:55.280
So your actual space used has
made a part of your memory

01:25:55.280 --> 01:25:58.040
nonusable because,
of course, these

01:25:58.040 --> 01:26:01.080
were the maximum output
you were expecting then.

01:26:01.080 --> 01:26:03.320
If a new query
comes in, no matter

01:26:03.320 --> 01:26:07.000
how small the prompt,
then the space remaining

01:26:07.000 --> 01:26:09.320
will be smaller than
the one you were

01:26:09.320 --> 01:26:11.680
expecting in the first place.

01:26:11.680 --> 01:26:14.000
So now, let's suppose
you do that at scale.

01:26:14.000 --> 01:26:18.100
You will end up with a bunch of
memory that is not being used,

01:26:18.100 --> 01:26:21.240
but at the same time
not being usable.

01:26:21.240 --> 01:26:28.680
And this is where the concept
of paged attention comes in.

01:26:28.680 --> 01:26:35.630
So you do not naively put
each key and value one

01:26:35.630 --> 01:26:36.530
after the other.

01:26:36.530 --> 01:26:41.150
But instead, you have some
logical view of your memory

01:26:41.150 --> 01:26:44.410
that is smart about
putting things around.

01:26:44.410 --> 01:26:48.390
So you would typically put
things into blocks of size B.

01:26:48.390 --> 01:26:52.830
And you maintain some metadata
that says for each query

01:26:52.830 --> 01:26:55.110
where you should put
each keys and values

01:26:55.110 --> 01:26:58.470
and where you should
look for them.

01:26:58.470 --> 01:27:00.790
So of course, you
have a trade-off here.

01:27:00.790 --> 01:27:04.870
So if your B is too
small, then your metadata

01:27:04.870 --> 01:27:06.130
is going to be huge.

01:27:06.130 --> 01:27:08.790
So you're going to tell
everyone, OK, you should go here

01:27:08.790 --> 01:27:09.290
and there.

01:27:09.290 --> 01:27:12.610
It's going to be quite a hassle.

01:27:12.610 --> 01:27:15.630
But at the same time, if you
have a B that's too high,

01:27:15.630 --> 01:27:17.510
then you're
reproducing the issue

01:27:17.510 --> 01:27:19.050
that you had in the first place.

01:27:19.050 --> 01:27:21.670
So you will see
still fragmentation.

01:27:21.670 --> 01:27:23.710
So just to give you
some intuition here,

01:27:23.710 --> 01:27:26.670
B is taken equal to 16.

01:27:26.670 --> 01:27:30.270
In practice, that's the
typical order of magnitude.

01:27:30.270 --> 01:27:32.750
OK, yeah.

01:27:32.750 --> 01:27:34.010
So the question is, what is B?

01:27:34.010 --> 01:27:35.870
B is the block size.

01:27:35.870 --> 01:27:39.630
So you have a lookup
map, a lookup table,

01:27:39.630 --> 01:27:42.370
and you want to see where
to store keys and values.

01:27:42.370 --> 01:27:46.190
And you store keys and values in
where that lookup map tells you.

01:27:46.190 --> 01:27:48.710
So let's say you have
a block of size 16.

01:27:48.710 --> 01:27:53.270
These are yours
and no one else's.

01:27:53.270 --> 01:27:54.910
So your fragmentation
issue can just

01:27:54.910 --> 01:27:59.270
be at the block level rather
than the whole memory level.

01:27:59.270 --> 01:28:01.950
Yeah, great question.

01:28:01.950 --> 01:28:04.450
So now, we're good on
fragmentation of the memory.

01:28:04.450 --> 01:28:06.430
Let's move on to the next topic.

01:28:06.430 --> 01:28:10.750
Now, we have too many
vectors to keep track of.

01:28:10.750 --> 01:28:12.530
So how we can remedy that?

01:28:12.530 --> 01:28:16.030
So we have seen a glimpse
at that in the past.

01:28:16.030 --> 01:28:20.570
So now, you operate
at an image A level.

01:28:20.570 --> 01:28:21.810
So multi-head attention.

01:28:21.810 --> 01:28:24.590
You suppose that you
have h heads and h

01:28:24.590 --> 01:28:28.590
projection of your queries
alongside h projection

01:28:28.590 --> 01:28:30.390
of your keys and values.

01:28:30.390 --> 01:28:35.020
This is something that you
could optimize by saying, OK,

01:28:35.020 --> 01:28:39.600
I do not want h projection
matrices of keys and values.

01:28:39.600 --> 01:28:41.460
I want g of them.

01:28:41.460 --> 01:28:45.780
And you combine
groups of queries

01:28:45.780 --> 01:28:49.900
inside each key and value.

01:28:49.900 --> 01:28:52.420
And this is what we call GQA.

01:28:52.420 --> 01:28:55.500
So I'm going to go a bit fast
on it because we have seen it.

01:28:55.500 --> 01:28:58.480
And you can have these
two extremal values.

01:28:58.480 --> 01:29:02.020
So if g equals h, then
you go back to MHA.

01:29:02.020 --> 01:29:05.540
And if g equals 1, meaning that
you have just one key and one

01:29:05.540 --> 01:29:10.180
value per token, you
go to multi-attention,

01:29:10.180 --> 01:29:12.620
which is typically another
extreme that you do not

01:29:12.620 --> 01:29:16.580
want because you do not have an
expression of keys and values

01:29:16.580 --> 01:29:18.700
that is expressive
enough for your model

01:29:18.700 --> 01:29:23.680
to build good enough features.

01:29:23.680 --> 01:29:25.020
So that's awesome.

01:29:25.020 --> 01:29:31.698
And just one word, if you take g
equals 2, and with h equals 24,

01:29:31.698 --> 01:29:33.490
I just want to give
some order of magnitude

01:29:33.490 --> 01:29:34.830
that's trivial to compute.

01:29:34.830 --> 01:29:37.110
It's 1 minus 2 over 24.

01:29:37.110 --> 01:29:40.530
So you have 91%
less memory taken.

01:29:40.530 --> 01:29:44.710
So just taking g
somewhere reasonable.

01:29:44.710 --> 01:29:49.890
So I think g equals 2 is what
was taken in Qwen3.8-Flash-Next.

01:29:49.890 --> 01:29:53.630
So it's very much something
that's used in practice.

01:29:53.630 --> 01:29:56.790
It's quite efficient.

01:29:59.410 --> 01:30:00.550
OK, great.

01:30:00.550 --> 01:30:02.530
And now-- yeah?

01:30:02.530 --> 01:30:04.425
So the comment is,
wouldn't this make

01:30:04.425 --> 01:30:06.050
the model less
expressive because there

01:30:06.050 --> 01:30:07.030
are less parameters?

01:30:07.030 --> 01:30:09.930
And absolutely,
this is a trade-off.

01:30:09.930 --> 01:30:14.770
So you would typically
do some grid search.

01:30:14.770 --> 01:30:17.250
Like, if you were a
pre-trainer of these models,

01:30:17.250 --> 01:30:19.770
you would do some grid
search, do ablation studies,

01:30:19.770 --> 01:30:24.090
see which g works best in order
to determine which one works.

01:30:24.090 --> 01:30:26.750
So it turns out in practice,
if you're too close to h,

01:30:26.750 --> 01:30:30.030
you have only marginal
improvements in performance.

01:30:30.030 --> 01:30:33.550
So this is why you
don't have h exactly.

01:30:33.550 --> 01:30:37.330
And the thing that you
mentioned is just good enough.

01:30:37.330 --> 01:30:39.050
But yeah, it's a trade-off.

01:30:39.050 --> 01:30:41.850
Yeah.

01:30:41.850 --> 01:30:42.430
OK, awesome.

01:30:42.430 --> 01:30:45.370
So I'm going to talk about the
technique that's not necessarily

01:30:45.370 --> 01:30:47.730
an and but maybe an or.

01:30:47.730 --> 01:30:50.670
So you see all these
vectors being stored.

01:30:50.670 --> 01:30:53.730
So you have g of them
per token now, g times 2

01:30:53.730 --> 01:30:55.410
for keys and values.

01:30:55.410 --> 01:30:58.630
But let's say you want less of
them, and you want them smaller.

01:30:58.630 --> 01:31:00.290
Then how do you do it?

01:31:00.290 --> 01:31:09.410
So you have this concept called
MLA, multi-latent attention.

01:31:09.410 --> 01:31:15.610
So what it does is that it wants
to convert all of these to 2h

01:31:15.610 --> 01:31:20.650
or 2g vectors into
just one that is small.

01:31:20.650 --> 01:31:24.330
So what we'll do is that
we'll apply a concept

01:31:24.330 --> 01:31:28.360
that we might have seen
Laura, where we decompose

01:31:28.360 --> 01:31:32.000
a matrix multiply into.

01:31:32.000 --> 01:31:35.720
So you have a smaller
representation

01:31:35.720 --> 01:31:37.600
that is being computed first.

01:31:37.600 --> 01:31:42.040
And the trick here is
to train then a decoder

01:31:42.040 --> 01:31:47.640
that you can use to reproduce
the representation of keys

01:31:47.640 --> 01:31:48.660
and values.

01:31:48.660 --> 01:31:51.720
And the latter ones
are not what you store.

01:31:51.720 --> 01:31:56.740
What you store are only these
shared and fixed representation.

01:32:00.280 --> 01:32:07.360
And the magic of MLA is that you
are sharing one of the matrices

01:32:07.360 --> 01:32:11.000
across keys and values, meaning
that your first projection

01:32:11.000 --> 01:32:14.360
matrix will give a single
representation that

01:32:14.360 --> 01:32:19.160
is shared across all heads
and both keys and values.

01:32:19.160 --> 01:32:25.730
So instead of being with a bunch
of vectors for keys and values,

01:32:25.730 --> 01:32:29.790
you're just with one per token.

01:32:29.790 --> 01:32:33.670
Quite amazing, no?

01:32:33.670 --> 01:32:34.490
OK, great.

01:32:34.490 --> 01:32:38.530
So just to do a recap, we
were in a bad situation.

01:32:38.530 --> 01:32:40.470
Now, we're in a good situation.

01:32:40.470 --> 01:32:44.190
Now, let's move on to-- so we're
discussing about the attention

01:32:44.190 --> 01:32:45.030
layer.

01:32:45.030 --> 01:32:48.710
Now, let's move on to exactly
the process of actually

01:32:48.710 --> 01:32:51.150
generating next tokens.

01:32:51.150 --> 01:32:54.170
So let's look at
the last layers.

01:32:54.170 --> 01:32:57.030
And I'm going to talk to
you about a concept that

01:32:57.030 --> 01:32:58.090
is quite powerful.

01:32:58.090 --> 01:33:01.670
So it's called
speculative decoding.

01:33:01.670 --> 01:33:05.470
Who has heard of
speculative decoding?

01:33:05.470 --> 01:33:06.410
Yeah, one?

01:33:06.410 --> 01:33:07.310
OK.

01:33:07.310 --> 01:33:10.350
I'm so glad we're
all seeing this

01:33:10.350 --> 01:33:12.050
because it's quite interesting.

01:33:12.050 --> 01:33:15.590
So the concept here,
so since we have

01:33:15.590 --> 01:33:19.110
said that the computation of
each new token at the time,

01:33:19.110 --> 01:33:21.830
the autoregression was
what taking us time,

01:33:21.830 --> 01:33:25.950
then why not generate
multiple of them

01:33:25.950 --> 01:33:28.750
and then use the property
that you have at prefill

01:33:28.750 --> 01:33:30.550
and also at training time?

01:33:30.550 --> 01:33:34.750
The fact that verifying
tokens is cheap

01:33:34.750 --> 01:33:38.110
because you can input them all
in parallel, but generating them

01:33:38.110 --> 01:33:39.750
is slow.

01:33:39.750 --> 01:33:42.430
So using these two
properties to our advantage

01:33:42.430 --> 01:33:47.270
to design a process that
makes generation cheaper,

01:33:47.270 --> 01:33:49.430
and it still
leverages verification

01:33:49.430 --> 01:33:52.630
because it's something that
we know isn't too expensive.

01:33:52.630 --> 01:33:54.630
So the concept here
is that you take

01:33:54.630 --> 01:33:58.670
a model called the draft model
to come here and generate

01:33:58.670 --> 01:34:01.270
for you next tokens.

01:34:01.270 --> 01:34:05.390
So d1 to dk, like
the next k tokens.

01:34:05.390 --> 01:34:11.230
And then you put all of them as
inputs alongside your prompt.

01:34:11.230 --> 01:34:16.430
And you use your target model
to verify some properties

01:34:16.430 --> 01:34:18.270
about them.

01:34:18.270 --> 01:34:19.750
So this is the gist.

01:34:19.750 --> 01:34:22.780
Now, it's going to
get more concrete.

01:34:22.780 --> 01:34:26.020
So here's the process
that I propose.

01:34:26.020 --> 01:34:31.780
So if the target is more
confident than the draft

01:34:31.780 --> 01:34:37.300
about the predicted token, then
the verification of that token

01:34:37.300 --> 01:34:42.540
accepts it, otherwise
accepts it with some ratio

01:34:42.540 --> 01:34:44.500
of probabilities.

01:34:44.500 --> 01:34:47.420
And if not, reject it.

01:34:47.420 --> 01:34:51.580
And if you reject it,
then sample the next token

01:34:51.580 --> 01:34:56.700
with the residual distribution
of a quantity that's

01:34:56.700 --> 01:35:00.980
displayed later on.

01:35:00.980 --> 01:35:06.240
And so this is called
rejection sampling.

01:35:06.240 --> 01:35:08.300
And in the process that
I mentioned to you,

01:35:08.300 --> 01:35:12.820
it's roughly us trying to
align the prediction with what

01:35:12.820 --> 01:35:14.923
the target says is most likely.

01:35:14.923 --> 01:35:16.340
So when the target
says it's super

01:35:16.340 --> 01:35:19.060
likely-- it says it's even
more likely than what the draft

01:35:19.060 --> 01:35:25.130
model says, accept it.
otherwise, have some rules.

01:35:25.130 --> 01:35:28.810
And when you convert
that into math notations,

01:35:28.810 --> 01:35:31.330
so you just have the--
the first case is

01:35:31.330 --> 01:35:33.150
if the probability is bigger.

01:35:33.150 --> 01:35:38.370
And then later on, when
I say relatively more,

01:35:38.370 --> 01:35:41.270
probability means more
probability of acceptance.

01:35:41.270 --> 01:35:45.570
It's nothing else than
the ratio of the two.

01:35:45.570 --> 01:35:51.610
So now, when I say speculative
decoding reproduces

01:35:51.610 --> 01:35:54.890
the probability distribution
of the target model,

01:35:54.890 --> 01:35:58.230
are you convinced that this
recipe will give you that?

01:36:01.110 --> 01:36:01.610
Yes?

01:36:01.610 --> 01:36:04.070
OK, so we have some
people who trust in me.

01:36:04.070 --> 01:36:06.690
Thank you.

01:36:06.690 --> 01:36:11.290
Does everyone agree that we will
recover the target distribution?

01:36:11.290 --> 01:36:12.410
I see some notes.

01:36:12.410 --> 01:36:14.390
Yeah, I agree with
you, it's not obvious.

01:36:14.390 --> 01:36:15.590
So let's prove it together.

01:36:23.574 --> 01:36:25.530
So let's see.

01:36:25.530 --> 01:36:30.850
So let's suppose that we
have a random variable called

01:36:30.850 --> 01:36:38.370
x that defines what your
next token is going to be.

01:36:38.370 --> 01:36:40.650
So just to make
sure that we agree,

01:36:40.650 --> 01:36:45.170
our goal is to prove
that the probability

01:36:45.170 --> 01:36:49.930
that this random variable
equals a given token x

01:36:49.930 --> 01:36:54.370
is the same as what your
target distribution says.

01:36:54.370 --> 01:36:56.890
Is that right?

01:36:56.890 --> 01:36:58.030
Awesome.

01:36:58.030 --> 01:36:59.350
So this is what we want.

01:36:59.350 --> 01:37:01.530
So goal.

01:37:01.530 --> 01:37:06.350
Now, let's try to compute it.

01:37:06.350 --> 01:37:08.370
So we have two branches here.

01:37:08.370 --> 01:37:12.950
So we have one that are
given by accepted cases.

01:37:12.950 --> 01:37:15.510
So when you see a check mark,
it's something that we accept.

01:37:15.510 --> 01:37:17.840
Otherwise, it's rejected.

01:37:17.840 --> 01:37:21.560
So I'm going to use the
law of total probability

01:37:21.560 --> 01:37:27.360
by saying that this probability
is the same as the one where

01:37:27.360 --> 01:37:35.240
we consider things that are
accepted plus the one where we

01:37:35.240 --> 01:37:38.660
consider a case of rejection.

01:37:43.000 --> 01:37:45.240
Does everyone agree?

01:37:45.240 --> 01:37:46.180
OK, great.

01:37:46.180 --> 01:37:54.960
Now, I'm going to use
joint probability formula.

01:37:54.960 --> 01:38:00.200
So if you say pa
intersect b, so it's

01:38:00.200 --> 01:38:04.400
p of a times p of b given a.

01:38:04.400 --> 01:38:07.280
Does everyone
recall that formula?

01:38:07.280 --> 01:38:11.560
So I'm going to do apply this
formula to the two terms here.

01:38:11.560 --> 01:38:13.800
So we're going to have--

01:38:13.800 --> 01:38:20.570
so let's say you're in a
situation of acceptance.

01:38:20.570 --> 01:38:25.590
So you are in a case where
the draft says something.

01:38:25.590 --> 01:38:27.610
And you accept it.

01:38:27.610 --> 01:38:34.110
So you say, OK, I accept the
draft that you've given me.

01:38:34.110 --> 01:38:37.550
So this is how I apply the
relationship for the first one.

01:38:37.550 --> 01:38:43.250
Then for the second one,
I do it in a similar way.

01:38:49.310 --> 01:38:55.290
So P rejected times P of x
equals small x given rejected.

01:38:58.950 --> 01:39:00.950
Does everyone agree?

01:39:00.950 --> 01:39:03.710
Now, let's compute
what these are.

01:39:03.710 --> 01:39:05.830
So these are
probability distribution

01:39:05.830 --> 01:39:07.370
of the next token for the draft.

01:39:07.370 --> 01:39:12.270
So this is nothing else than D
of x probability of acceptance

01:39:12.270 --> 01:39:13.760
given d equals x.

01:39:13.760 --> 01:39:15.300
So it's written on the board.

01:39:15.300 --> 01:39:20.420
So it's the minimum of 1
and T of x over D of x.

01:39:20.420 --> 01:39:21.600
Does everyone agree?

01:39:34.740 --> 01:39:36.020
All right.

01:39:36.020 --> 01:39:40.940
And then this, since everything
is positive and nice,

01:39:40.940 --> 01:39:52.940
this is the minimum of D of x
and T of x for the left term.

01:39:52.940 --> 01:39:55.180
OK, great.

01:39:55.180 --> 01:39:57.840
Now, let's deal
with the right term.

01:40:22.820 --> 01:40:24.560
So p rejected.

01:40:29.540 --> 01:40:34.640
So I'm going to say
it's 1 minus p accepted.

01:40:39.300 --> 01:40:42.180
And then p accepted,
it's nothing

01:40:42.180 --> 01:40:49.080
else than the sum of the
cases where x equals x prime.

01:40:52.540 --> 01:40:59.180
And where this is accepted,
summed over all x primes.

01:40:59.180 --> 01:41:01.860
Do you agree?

01:41:01.860 --> 01:41:03.620
Great.

01:41:03.620 --> 01:41:07.660
And now, each of these terms,
we have taken the effort

01:41:07.660 --> 01:41:09.770
of nicely computing it for you.

01:41:09.770 --> 01:41:20.510
So it's 1 minus the sum of
mean of Dx prime, Tx prime.

01:41:24.170 --> 01:41:26.610
And then what is 1?

01:41:26.610 --> 01:41:29.490
1 is the sum--

01:41:29.490 --> 01:41:32.770
so let's just see
what we get first.

01:41:32.770 --> 01:41:37.170
So this is the same
as me saying that this

01:41:37.170 --> 01:41:45.990
is plus the max between minus of
D of x prime minus T of x prime.

01:41:50.730 --> 01:41:53.170
And then here, I'm
going to do a trick.

01:41:53.170 --> 01:41:58.250
1 is the sum over x
prime of T of x prime.

01:42:03.750 --> 01:42:08.960
Such that when I
sum the two, I'm

01:42:08.960 --> 01:42:12.040
going to get a sum
over all x primes

01:42:12.040 --> 01:42:24.840
of the max of Tx prime
minus D of x prime and 0.

01:42:24.840 --> 01:42:28.400
Does everyone agree?

01:42:28.400 --> 01:42:33.240
So I went through all
this trouble for you

01:42:33.240 --> 01:42:42.600
such that we can
say that this is

01:42:42.600 --> 01:42:45.840
nothing else than the residual
distribution that I mentioned.

01:42:45.840 --> 01:42:54.680
So it's T of x minus D of x
over the sum of the same--

01:42:54.680 --> 01:42:59.760
so it's the max, of
course, of this quantity

01:42:59.760 --> 01:43:05.920
with 0 over the sum over
x primes of max of 0

01:43:05.920 --> 01:43:12.120
and T of x prime
minus D of x prime.

01:43:12.120 --> 01:43:15.400
And I've gone through
all this trouble for us

01:43:15.400 --> 01:43:19.240
to be able to cancel
these terms out.

01:43:19.240 --> 01:43:23.840
Are you convinced
such that at the end

01:43:23.840 --> 01:43:29.060
you're with minimum
of D of x, T of x.

01:43:31.920 --> 01:43:39.540
Plus the maximum between
0 and T of x minus D of x.

01:43:47.640 --> 01:43:52.480
And this is what
we want to check

01:43:52.480 --> 01:43:56.520
that it is equal to T of x.

01:43:56.520 --> 01:44:01.460
So if you do the
distinction of cases,

01:44:01.460 --> 01:44:05.470
depending on D of x being bigger
than T of x, T of x being bigger

01:44:05.470 --> 01:44:06.610
than D of x.

01:44:06.610 --> 01:44:08.430
You see that it is the case.

01:44:08.430 --> 01:44:09.450
So congrats, everyone.

01:44:09.450 --> 01:44:12.350
We have proven this
fact that was nontrivial

01:44:12.350 --> 01:44:15.470
that some people knew was true.

01:44:15.470 --> 01:44:18.070
Good job.

01:44:18.070 --> 01:44:19.850
So this is why it's amazing.

01:44:19.850 --> 01:44:21.430
And the second
thing that's great

01:44:21.430 --> 01:44:26.790
is when you do this concept
of generating then verifying,

01:44:26.790 --> 01:44:29.550
you get the next token for
free because everything

01:44:29.550 --> 01:44:35.190
that you put in the target
model gets computed,

01:44:35.190 --> 01:44:39.870
and you get the next
token, which is amazing.

01:44:39.870 --> 01:44:42.930
And the other thing
that I want to say,

01:44:42.930 --> 01:44:44.570
so you see these
rejection cases?

01:44:44.570 --> 01:44:46.950
These are those that we
want to avoid at all costs

01:44:46.950 --> 01:44:49.430
because they mean
that we exit the loop.

01:44:49.430 --> 01:44:53.510
And everything that we have done
with the draft after that point

01:44:53.510 --> 01:44:55.090
is going to be wasted work.

01:44:55.090 --> 01:44:57.370
So we do not want
to do wasted work.

01:44:57.370 --> 01:45:00.190
We want these distributions
to be aligned,

01:45:00.190 --> 01:45:02.470
which is my next point.

01:45:02.470 --> 01:45:06.780
And you might have heard
of this concept called

01:45:06.780 --> 01:45:10.220
knowledge distillation, where
you take a smaller model.

01:45:10.220 --> 01:45:13.140
So this draft 1
that we have defined

01:45:13.140 --> 01:45:16.920
to fit the predictions
of your teacher model.

01:45:16.920 --> 01:45:18.980
And the nice
property is that you

01:45:18.980 --> 01:45:22.380
learn the distribution of
the next token, which is what

01:45:22.380 --> 01:45:24.460
would align the distributions.

01:45:24.460 --> 01:45:26.100
So this is one thing you can do.

01:45:26.100 --> 01:45:29.500
A second thing you can do
is by using a method called

01:45:29.500 --> 01:45:34.820
multi-token prediction,
where you use the same LLM

01:45:34.820 --> 01:45:40.060
both as draft and as verifier.

01:45:40.060 --> 01:45:44.360
So instead of training just
one linear and softmax heads,

01:45:44.360 --> 01:45:46.620
you train k ones.

01:45:46.620 --> 01:45:48.980
And you use these k
ones for your draft.

01:45:48.980 --> 01:45:52.260
And you use just your
first one as your verifier,

01:45:52.260 --> 01:45:54.460
which is quite nice.

01:45:54.460 --> 01:45:57.860
So in one minute,
answering the two questions

01:45:57.860 --> 01:46:00.180
that Afshine had mentioned.

01:46:00.180 --> 01:46:02.260
How do we make everything fit?

01:46:02.260 --> 01:46:06.180
So paged attention is
what will make us fit

01:46:06.180 --> 01:46:08.300
these things into our hardware.

01:46:08.300 --> 01:46:11.840
But then methods, such as
reducing the number of heads

01:46:11.840 --> 01:46:14.060
or even computing
representations that

01:46:14.060 --> 01:46:16.740
are small enough, will help us.

01:46:16.740 --> 01:46:22.180
And regarding making it faster,
generating more tokens at once

01:46:22.180 --> 01:46:24.140
is what will help us.

01:46:24.140 --> 01:46:26.820
And I just want to
conclude with the fact

01:46:26.820 --> 01:46:28.980
that everything
that we saw today,

01:46:28.980 --> 01:46:31.300
while more tedious
than usual because you

01:46:31.300 --> 01:46:34.180
have to talk about
technical stuff much more,

01:46:34.180 --> 01:46:35.640
they are top of mind.

01:46:35.640 --> 01:46:38.720
So this is the home page
or artificial in analysis,

01:46:38.720 --> 01:46:40.500
an aggregator of benchmarks.

01:46:40.500 --> 01:46:45.180
So the top of the home page,
2/3 of the real estate is taken

01:46:45.180 --> 01:46:49.160
by these
inference-related graphs.

01:46:49.160 --> 01:46:52.660
So this is important and also
top of mind to you as users.

01:46:52.660 --> 01:46:56.150
And with that, I hope you
have a great Halloween.

